WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.994 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:00.994 --> 00:00:01.982 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:01.982 --> 00:00:04.452 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.452 --> 00:00:09.700 align:middle line:90%


00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.520 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So today we're going
to consider an application

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.840 align:middle line:90%
of backward induction.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.300 align:middle line:84%
Today is going to be
about negotiation.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.360 align:middle line:90%


00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.920 align:middle line:84%
And the basic idea is that
when two parties are bargaining

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.040 align:middle line:84%
or negotiating, they often--
one party might make an offer

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.180 align:middle line:90%
and say, let's do this.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.680 align:middle line:84%
And then the other party might
respond with a counteroffer,

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.820 align:middle line:84%
and they might go
back and forth.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.700 align:middle line:84%
So right now the
government just shut down.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.800 align:middle line:84%
That's exactly what's
happening between Trump

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.380 align:middle line:90%
and the Democratic Party.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.360 align:middle line:84%
They might be countering
offers and go back and forth

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.160 align:middle line:84%
and see if they can
eventually reach an agreement.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.360 align:middle line:84%
So the first model--
we're going to discuss

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.160 align:middle line:90%
two models of negotiation.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.180 align:middle line:84%
One is going to be about
pretrial settlements.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:00.570 align:middle line:90%


00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.950 align:middle line:84%
And the other is going to
be about price haggling.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.097 align:middle line:90%


00:01:07.097 --> 00:01:09.430 align:middle line:84%
And I think these are kind
of illustrative applications.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.530 align:middle line:84%
Of course, we're not going
to include all the details

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.447 align:middle line:90%
of these applications.

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:15.150 align:middle line:84%
We're going to introduce
foundational stylized models.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.610 align:middle line:84%
But I think they can capture
some important elements

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:18.990 align:middle line:90%
of these issues.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.450 align:middle line:90%
So let's start with settlements.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.190 align:middle line:84%
So in many cases,
in criminal cases,

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:29.050 align:middle line:84%
we often have pretrial
plea bargain bargaining.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:33.170 align:middle line:84%
And in civil cases, we
often have the two parties

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.130 align:middle line:84%
working together before the case
and trying to reach a settlement

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.770 align:middle line:90%
to avoid trial.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.930 align:middle line:84%
So today, let's think
about civil cases.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.290 align:middle line:84%
I think that fits
the model best.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:48.010 align:middle line:84%
And in practice, a lot of civil
cases never make it to trial.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:49.810 align:middle line:84%
The two parties
reach some settlement

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.590 align:middle line:90%
before the case goes to trial.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.130 align:middle line:84%
What are some reasons
why cases like this

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.510 align:middle line:90%
often don't go to trial?

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.970 align:middle line:84%
Why do we see so many
pretrial settlements?

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.210 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts?

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.235 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Both sides want
to avoid having legal fees.

00:02:03.235 --> 00:02:04.110 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Legal fees.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.710 align:middle line:84%
So legal costs are
a big part of it.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.650 align:middle line:84%
And that's really
what our model today

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.170 align:middle line:84%
is going to focus on, that
having a drawn-out trial

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.010 align:middle line:90%
is very costly.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.370 align:middle line:84%
Did you have the same
idea or any other--

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.250 align:middle line:90%
same idea.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:15.870 align:middle line:90%
Anyone else?

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.130 align:middle line:90%
Any other reasons?

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.590 align:middle line:90%
It could be legal costs.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.570 align:middle line:84%
It could just be the
cost of delay, which is

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:23.910 align:middle line:90%
similar but slightly different.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:27.170 align:middle line:84%
It could be that even aside
from paying the lawyers,

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.170 align:middle line:84%
if we're going to be stuck
in court for so long,

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.570 align:middle line:84%
and it's going to delay our
productive activity, that

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.290 align:middle line:90%
might be costly.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:39.270 align:middle line:84%
Another big one could
be risk aversion.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.250 align:middle line:84%
This is not going to
be in our model today.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.990 align:middle line:84%
But it might be that I think
that if we go to court,

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.050 align:middle line:84%
there's a chance I
win tons of money.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.770 align:middle line:84%
There's a chance I
don't win anything.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.570 align:middle line:84%
So I'd rather
accept a settlement

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.890 align:middle line:84%
where I win some small
intermediate amount of money

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.210 align:middle line:90%
with certainty.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.740 align:middle line:84%
So risk aversion, and
I should be clear here,

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.120 align:middle line:90%
over monetary payoffs.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.260 align:middle line:90%


00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.420 align:middle line:84%
I think this is also
true in criminal cases

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.300 align:middle line:84%
where someone might
think, well, there's

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.180 align:middle line:90%
a chance I get a life sentence.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.540 align:middle line:84%
I'd rather accept a
plea bargain where

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.720 align:middle line:84%
I'm certain to go to
prison for five years,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.100 align:middle line:84%
rather than have a risk that
I serve a life sentence.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.460 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe one final
one that we often see

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:24.580 align:middle line:84%
is about privacy and maybe
embarrassing information

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:29.280 align:middle line:84%
coming out, or potentially trade
secrets being revealed at trial.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.238 align:middle line:90%


00:03:35.238 --> 00:03:37.280 align:middle line:84%
There are some provisions
to try to prevent this,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.660 align:middle line:84%
but I think there's
still some risk of this.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.380 align:middle line:84%
So today, we're going to
introduce a formal model that

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:49.420 align:middle line:84%
can try to make some predictions
about what kind of settlements

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.140 align:middle line:84%
we'll see and how long
negotiations will last.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.480 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this model isn't
going to capture everything,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.500 align:middle line:84%
but it's going to be a good
starting point for today.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.000 align:middle line:84%
So in our model,
we're going to have

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.400 align:middle line:84%
two parties who we call the
plaintiff and the defendant.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.900 align:middle line:90%


00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.498 align:middle line:84%
So the plaintiff is the party
that's suing someone else.

00:04:07.498 --> 00:04:09.540 align:middle line:84%
So the plaintiff feels
that they've been wronged,

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.260 align:middle line:84%
and they're seeking a
monetary settlement.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.740 align:middle line:84%
The defendant has been
accused of wronging someone,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.300 align:middle line:84%
and they're being
potentially asked

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:18.680 align:middle line:90%
to pay a monetary settlement.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.524 align:middle line:90%
So let's be clear.

00:04:19.524 --> 00:04:20.899 align:middle line:84%
At the end, there's
going to be--

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:25.540 align:middle line:84%
if they reach some settlement,
that's an amount of money that's

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.940 align:middle line:84%
going to go from the
defendant to the plaintiff.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.980 align:middle line:84%
So from-- maybe I'll just
write it with arrows--

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.660 align:middle line:90%
D to P.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.820 align:middle line:84%
So just to make sure
we're on the same page,

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:41.380 align:middle line:84%
the defendant is hoping to pay
out a very small settlement.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.980 align:middle line:84%
The defendant
doesn't want to have

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:47.060 align:middle line:84%
to pay a big fine or a big
punishment to the plaintiff.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.200 align:middle line:84%
The plaintiff is seeking
a very large settlement

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.030 align:middle line:84%
because they want to get
paid out as much as possible.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:56.190 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, we're going
to make a few simplifying

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:57.310 align:middle line:90%
assumptions.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.910 align:middle line:84%
One of them is that
there's no uncertainty

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.610 align:middle line:90%
about the outcome of a trial.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:09.413 align:middle line:90%


00:05:09.413 --> 00:05:10.330 align:middle line:90%
Why are we doing this?

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:13.325 align:middle line:84%
Well, we want to isolate
the effect of legal costs.

00:05:13.325 --> 00:05:14.950 align:middle line:84%
In this model, we're
not going to worry

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.390 align:middle line:84%
about risk aversion and the
potential uncertainties that

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:18.290 align:middle line:90%
come with trial.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.707 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going to assume
here, to make things simple,

00:05:20.707 --> 00:05:26.310 align:middle line:84%
is that if they go to
trial, the settlement amount

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.890 align:middle line:84%
is known to be--
will definitely be J.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.238 align:middle line:90%
And we'll use to indicate judge.

00:05:30.238 --> 00:05:31.530 align:middle line:90%
This is what the judge decides.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:35.190 align:middle line:84%
So both parties know if we
go to trial the amount that's

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.370 align:middle line:90%
going to be agreed upon.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:37.953 align:middle line:84%
So here, we're not
thinking, you might

00:05:37.953 --> 00:05:41.150 align:middle line:84%
imagine the judge deciding
whether the plaintiff--

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.170 align:middle line:84%
whether the defendant did
anything wrong at all.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.230 align:middle line:84%
Here, we're imagining
that yeah, the defendant

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:46.688 align:middle line:84%
has certainly done
something wrong.

00:05:46.688 --> 00:05:49.680 align:middle line:84%
The question is only how
much they have to pay.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.260 align:middle line:84%
And here, the assumption is
that if they go to court,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.920 align:middle line:84%
the amount of the settlement
will be some fixed amount, J.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.000 align:middle line:84%
And then we need to specify what
the cost of going to court are.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.200 align:middle line:84%
So maybe the trial
costs are going

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:07.937 align:middle line:84%
to be Cp for the plaintiff
and Cd for the defendant.

00:06:07.937 --> 00:06:10.020 align:middle line:84%
And I think in the notes
they use capital letters.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.480 align:middle line:84%
But I find at the board
it's hard to know what's

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:12.938 align:middle line:84%
capital and lowercase,
so I'll just

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:17.200 align:middle line:84%
use a hat to indicate the
costs if we go to trial.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:21.200 align:middle line:84%
But then each period that we
negotiate before the trial also

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:21.840 align:middle line:90%
has costs.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.860 align:middle line:84%
You don't just pay the
lawyers when you go to trial.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.277 align:middle line:84%
If you want to try to reach a
settlement, that, in itself,

00:06:26.277 --> 00:06:26.880 align:middle line:90%
is costly.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.520 align:middle line:84%
So we're also going to assume
that there are per period--

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.760 align:middle line:84%
and that will be clear when
I describe the periods--

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:40.260 align:middle line:84%
per period negotiation or
pretrial negotiation costs.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:44.080 align:middle line:90%


00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.160 align:middle line:84%
And these are just going to
be cp and cd without hats.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.720 align:middle line:84%
So each period that
we negotiate for,

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.140 align:middle line:84%
the plaintiff has to pay a
cost of cp to their lawyers,

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.000 align:middle line:84%
and the defendant pays a
cost, cd, to their lawyers.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.920 align:middle line:84%
So now let's describe the
alternating offer structure

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:02.920 align:middle line:90%
of this game.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:06.300 align:middle line:84%
This is a very common format
that we see in reality.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.180 align:middle line:90%
You make some offer.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.397 align:middle line:84%
The other side will either
say it's acceptable,

00:07:10.397 --> 00:07:12.480 align:middle line:84%
or they might reject your
offer and then come back

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.680 align:middle line:84%
with a counteroffer that's
more favorable to them.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.880 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to imagine that
we have time going-- we have 0,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.640 align:middle line:84%
period 1, period 2, period 3,
all the way up to, let's say,

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:33.120 align:middle line:84%
2n minus 1, 2n minus
1, and then 2n.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.680 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll
imagine is that it's

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.440 align:middle line:84%
known that the date
of the trial is set.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:38.580 align:middle line:90%
So this is the trial.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.160 align:middle line:90%


00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:47.130 align:middle line:84%
So we know that the trial is
going to occur in period 2n.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:48.588 align:middle line:90%
And if we go to trial--

00:07:48.588 --> 00:07:50.130 align:middle line:84%
when I say it will
occur, what I mean

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.373 align:middle line:84%
is if we don't reach a
settlement before then,

00:07:52.373 --> 00:07:54.790 align:middle line:84%
then the trial occurs, and we
know what's going to happen.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:56.540 align:middle line:84%
The settlement is going
to be J, and we're

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.730 align:middle line:84%
going to have to pay
all these legal costs.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:00.270 align:middle line:84%
But in anticipation
of the trial,

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:05.170 align:middle line:84%
what we're imagining is
there's time to have n rounds,

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:08.730 align:middle line:84%
or 2n minus 2n periods, but
n total rounds of alternating

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:09.650 align:middle line:90%
offers.

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:14.410 align:middle line:84%
So what we imagine is
in the first period,

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:19.410 align:middle line:84%
the plaintiff is going to be
the one who's going to propose.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:20.930 align:middle line:90%
So it's a little confusing.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:22.350 align:middle line:90%
P is for plaintiff.

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:23.250 align:middle line:90%
You're not proposer.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:31.290 align:middle line:84%
But the plaintiff, maybe I'll
say offers, I'll call this S0.

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:35.122 align:middle line:84%
So in period 0, they're going
to say, look, let's just settle.

00:08:35.122 --> 00:08:37.289 align:middle line:84%
And the amount that you're
going to have to pay me--

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:38.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm the plaintiff,
you're the defendant.

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.250 align:middle line:84%
The amount that you're going
to have to pay me is S0.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.690 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to
accept this or not?

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:46.630 align:middle line:84%
And if it's accepted, then
you pay me this amount.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:47.330 align:middle line:90%
We're done.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.130 align:middle line:84%
And if it's rejected,
we continue.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:56.670 align:middle line:84%
So then the defendant can choose
either to accept or reject.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:00.570 align:middle line:90%


00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.690 align:middle line:84%
And if the defendant
accepts this offer,

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:03.930 align:middle line:90%
well, now we're done.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:05.650 align:middle line:90%
The defendant pays S0.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:06.790 align:middle line:90%
We never go to court.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.330 align:middle line:90%
The game is over.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:10.163 align:middle line:84%
But if they reject this
offer, well, they're

00:09:10.163 --> 00:09:11.205 align:middle line:90%
not just going to reject.

00:09:11.205 --> 00:09:13.170 align:middle line:84%
They're going to come
back tomorrow and propose

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.410 align:middle line:90%
a counteroffer.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:16.290 align:middle line:84%
So in the next
period, the defendant

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.512 align:middle line:90%
is going to make an offer.

00:09:18.512 --> 00:09:20.970 align:middle line:84%
And now I'll call that offer--
they can make any offer they

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:21.510 align:middle line:90%
want--

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:23.130 align:middle line:90%
S1.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:26.490 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to
say, let's settle now,

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:29.810 align:middle line:84%
and I'll pay you S1
dollars if you accept it.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:32.150 align:middle line:84%
And again now it's
the plaintiff's turn

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:33.570 align:middle line:90%
who's going to say--

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:41.420 align:middle line:84%
who's going to choose
either to accept or reject.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:46.400 align:middle line:84%
And we can see how this is
going to keep going until--

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:46.900 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:50.260 align:middle line:84%
In even periods, the plaintiff
is making the offers.

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:52.440 align:middle line:90%
So 2n minus 2 is an even number.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:54.420 align:middle line:84%
So that means here,
the plaintiff,

00:09:54.420 --> 00:10:00.140 align:middle line:84%
if we get to this point,
would make an offer,

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:03.020 align:middle line:90%
let's say, s2n minus 2.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.700 align:middle line:84%
And again, in this
case, the defendant

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:07.460 align:middle line:84%
is going to choose
to accept or reject.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:09.740 align:middle line:90%
If it's rejected, we move here.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.300 align:middle line:90%
Now it's the defendant's turn.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:14.020 align:middle line:84%
They're going to make
this last offer--

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:18.340 align:middle line:84%
S2n minus 1, and
the plaintiff is

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:22.700 align:middle line:84%
going to choose whether to
accept or reject that offer.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.700 align:middle line:84%
And this is only00 and
only in this last period

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:25.575 align:middle line:90%
are things different.

00:10:25.575 --> 00:10:29.320 align:middle line:84%
So in this last period, if
the offer is accepted, great.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.620 align:middle line:90%
It's over as before.

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:33.360 align:middle line:84%
But if it's rejected, well,
now the trial is here.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.240 align:middle line:84%
So instead of going to one
more round of negotiation,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.100 align:middle line:84%
we just go to trial,
and we see what

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.440 align:middle line:90%
the outcomes are going to be.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.070 align:middle line:90%


00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:48.870 align:middle line:84%
So just so we understand the
costs, the Cp hat and Cd hat

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:51.310 align:middle line:84%
cost, the trial costs,
are only realized

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:53.630 align:middle line:90%
if we actually go to trial.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:58.190 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, the per period
negotiation costs

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:01.470 align:middle line:84%
are realized in every period
until we reach a settlement.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:04.790 align:middle line:90%
So here, we have Cp Cd.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:08.990 align:middle line:90%
Here, we have Cp Cd and so on.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:13.825 align:middle line:84%
So just so we understand, if we
were to settle in this period 0,

00:11:13.825 --> 00:11:15.950 align:middle line:84%
we would have only had to
negotiate for exactly one

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:16.770 align:middle line:90%
period.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.030 align:middle line:84%
So we'd each bear
these costs only once.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:22.030 align:middle line:84%
But if we settled in, say, the
third period, period three,

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:24.010 align:middle line:84%
well, it's tricky
because we started at 0.

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:25.630 align:middle line:84%
So if we settled
here, then we've

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:29.050 align:middle line:84%
had to negotiate
1, 2, 3, 4 periods.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:31.750 align:middle line:84%
And that means we'll each
have to pay four times our

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.830 align:middle line:90%
per period negotiation costs.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.950 align:middle line:84%
If we were to all
the way go to trial,

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.250 align:middle line:84%
we're going to have to
pay our trial costs,

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:42.250 align:middle line:84%
and we've negotiated
each of these periods.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:46.870 align:middle line:84%
So we'll each have to pay 2n for
the 2n negotiation periods times

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:48.810 align:middle line:84%
our per period
negotiation costs,

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:53.390 align:middle line:90%
plus the cost of going to trial.

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:54.750 align:middle line:90%
So this is the game.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:58.470 align:middle line:84%
And now what we'd like to
do is analyze this game

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:01.110 align:middle line:90%
using backward induction.

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:05.030 align:middle line:84%
Any questions on the
setup of the game?

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:09.150 align:middle line:84%
So let's first just recall
what backward induction

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.990 align:middle line:90%
is going to deliver for us.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.750 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to apply
backward induction.

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:20.410 align:middle line:84%
What this is going to give us
is a special Nash equilibrium.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:24.710 align:middle line:90%


00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:26.810 align:middle line:84%
Remember, first
we have to think,

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:29.270 align:middle line:84%
are we allowed to apply
backward induction?

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:30.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, backward
induction only can

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:34.550 align:middle line:84%
be applied to games with
extensive form games

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.600 align:middle line:90%
with perfect information.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:38.558 align:middle line:84%
And here, we have
perfect information

00:12:38.558 --> 00:12:40.600 align:middle line:84%
because there's no point
in the negotiation where

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:43.640 align:middle line:84%
someone doesn't know what
someone else did previously.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:45.460 align:middle line:84%
Everything is
observed in this game.

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:48.000 align:middle line:84%
So we're allowed to
apply backward induction.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.680 align:middle line:84%
The problem with
games like this is

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:52.200 align:middle line:84%
that there are many, many
Nash equilibria that may

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:55.320 align:middle line:90%
involve non-credible threats.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.080 align:middle line:84%
So backward induction is going
to give us this very special

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.380 align:middle line:90%
Nash equilibrium.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:02.960 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes the term we use--
maybe another way of saying

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.500 align:middle line:84%
special is it's going to
be a Nash equilibrium,

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:07.840 align:middle line:84%
but it's also going to have
this property that's often

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:09.660 align:middle line:90%
called sequential optimality.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:15.440 align:middle line:90%


00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:17.760 align:middle line:84%
Remember, our key
observation from last class

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.960 align:middle line:84%
was that Nash
equilibrium by itself

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.940 align:middle line:84%
doesn't necessarily imply
sequential optimality.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:27.200 align:middle line:84%
That is, it may be under
some Nash equilibria

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:30.760 align:middle line:84%
that players are behaving
suboptimally at contingencies

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:33.080 align:middle line:90%
that are not reached.

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:36.520 align:middle line:84%
And sequential optimality
imposes the stronger condition

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:42.380 align:middle line:84%
that we have optimal play
at every contingency.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:47.160 align:middle line:90%


00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:49.280 align:middle line:84%
Particular, even
those contingencies

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:50.340 align:middle line:90%
that are not reached.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:58.760 align:middle line:90%


00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:00.360 align:middle line:84%
So Nash equilibrium
is already going

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:02.760 align:middle line:84%
to take care of the
contingencies that we do reach.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:05.880 align:middle line:84%
Nash equilibrium is already
going to require optimal play

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.480 align:middle line:90%
at those contingencies.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.240 align:middle line:84%
But Nash equilibrium
doesn't actually

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:13.240 align:middle line:84%
impose many constraints on
these unreached contingencies.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.840 align:middle line:84%
And what sequential
optimality does, or requires,

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.520 align:middle line:84%
is that players are
playing optimal even there.

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:22.040 align:middle line:84%
Because if players were
not playing optimal

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:24.560 align:middle line:84%
at some contingency,
that would capture

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:26.373 align:middle line:84%
what we think of as
a noncredible threat.

00:14:26.373 --> 00:14:28.040 align:middle line:84%
That would mean a
player was threatening

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.260 align:middle line:84%
to behave in such a way that
would not be optimal for them

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:33.090 align:middle line:84%
or credible for them
to follow through

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:35.950 align:middle line:84%
on if that contingency
were actually reached.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:38.570 align:middle line:90%


00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:41.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, a key thing to
keep in mind here

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:44.530 align:middle line:84%
is that what backward induction
gives us is a Nash equilibrium.

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:47.050 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to give
us a strategy profile.

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:49.930 align:middle line:84%
So often, when people
analyze a game like this,

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:54.590 align:middle line:84%
it's tempting to say, oh the
answer is we settle in period 5,

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:56.610 align:middle line:84%
and this is the
amount of payments.

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:58.770 align:middle line:84%
That's an outcome of
the game, but that's not

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.830 align:middle line:84%
a complete description
of the strategies.

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:02.770 align:middle line:84%
So a Nash equilibrium
is going to have

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.650 align:middle line:84%
to specify the player's
complete contingent plans

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:06.710 align:middle line:90%
throughout the game.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:08.930 align:middle line:84%
And that means that the
strategies in games like this

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:11.610 align:middle line:84%
are much, much more complicated
than the actual outcome that's

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:13.210 align:middle line:84%
reached, which might
just be we settle

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:15.610 align:middle line:90%
for this amount in this period.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.075 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's get into it.

00:15:17.075 --> 00:15:18.450 align:middle line:84%
It's backward
induction, so we're

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:21.170 align:middle line:84%
going to work backwards,
not surprisingly.

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:24.610 align:middle line:84%
And let's start
with-- well, you might

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:26.030 align:middle line:90%
say we're working backwards.

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.770 align:middle line:84%
Let's start in
period 2n minus 1.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.630 align:middle line:84%
But actually, even
within period 2n minus 1,

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.410 align:middle line:90%
we have sequential moves.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:36.330 align:middle line:84%
First, the defendant
makes an offer,

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:38.012 align:middle line:90%
and then the proposer responds.

00:15:38.012 --> 00:15:39.970 align:middle line:84%
So if we want to work
backwards, we're actually

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.030 align:middle line:84%
going to split this
last period up into two,

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:46.290 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to work in the
second part of the last period.

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:56.210 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to say suppose
we're in period 2n minus 1.

00:15:56.210 --> 00:16:00.750 align:middle line:84%
And the contingency we're
at as the plaintiff,

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:05.650 align:middle line:84%
is that we're facing some
offer from the defendant.

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:12.970 align:middle line:84%
So given some
offer, S2n minus 1--

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:14.990 align:middle line:84%
this is an offer
by the defendant--

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:17.530 align:middle line:90%


00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:19.190 align:middle line:90%
the proposer has two choices.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:22.010 align:middle line:90%


00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:26.850 align:middle line:84%
They can either
accept the offer,

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:29.860 align:middle line:90%
or they can reject the offer.

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:33.340 align:middle line:84%
I should point out here that
I'm being a little sloppy.

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:36.460 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying suppose
the proposer receives

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:38.820 align:middle line:90%
an offer of S2n minus 1.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:42.000 align:middle line:84%
But technically, this doesn't
specify the full contingency.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.580 align:middle line:84%
What would I need to
specify if I wanted to say--

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:46.620 align:middle line:84%
if I were to write out
this whole game tree,

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:48.840 align:middle line:84%
and I want to figure out
where I am in the game tree,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.840 align:middle line:84%
what is this contingency going
to specify within the tree?

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:56.140 align:middle line:90%


00:16:56.140 --> 00:17:00.860 align:middle line:84%
It's not just enough to specify
the offer in the last period.

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:01.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.380 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Maybe that you need
to know everyone before?

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:04.339 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Everything
that happened before.

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:07.079 align:middle line:84%
Because we know in a tree, when
we're at a node in the tree,

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:09.380 align:middle line:84%
that node tells us the
entire path through the tree.

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:12.060 align:middle line:84%
So technically, a contingency
would be something

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:15.819 align:middle line:84%
like, the plaintiff made this
offer in the zeroth period,

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:17.118 align:middle line:90%
and it was rejected.

00:17:17.118 --> 00:17:19.160 align:middle line:84%
The defendant made this
offer in the next period.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:20.000 align:middle line:90%
It was rejected.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.660 align:middle line:84%
The plaintiff made this offer,
and it was rejected, and so on.

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:25.500 align:middle line:84%
But what I implicitly
mean here is

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.589 align:middle line:84%
there's a lot of
different contingencies,

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:29.470 align:middle line:84%
but the incentives
of the plaintiff

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:32.070 align:middle line:84%
are going to be the same at
all the contingencies where

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.370 align:middle line:90%
this is the offer that's made.

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:35.328 align:middle line:84%
So I'm basically grouping
all the contingencies

00:17:35.328 --> 00:17:37.430 align:middle line:84%
in my head and reasoning
about all of them

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:38.850 align:middle line:90%
together at the same time.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:42.633 align:middle line:84%
Because at this point, if
you're offering me S2n minus 1,

00:17:42.633 --> 00:17:45.050 align:middle line:84%
I don't really care what you
offered me three periods ago.

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:46.050 align:middle line:90%
That's not on the table.

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:48.050 align:middle line:84%
So it's not relevant
to my optimal decision.

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:49.790 align:middle line:84%
But if I wanted to
be really precise,

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:52.110 align:middle line:84%
I could, in principle,
behave differently

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:54.950 align:middle line:90%
at different contingencies.

00:17:54.950 --> 00:17:58.030 align:middle line:84%
So now let's carefully
write down my payoffs.

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:03.110 align:middle line:84%
If I accept this offer
as the plaintiff,

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:05.870 align:middle line:84%
what are my payoffs going
to be, my total payoffs,

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:08.170 align:middle line:84%
taking into account the
settlement, my legal costs,

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:08.930 align:middle line:90%
all these things?

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:15.970 align:middle line:90%


00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:16.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:24.785 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] be s2n
minus 1 minus Cp times 2n S2.

00:18:24.785 --> 00:18:25.410 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:29.150 align:middle line:84%
I think 2n minus 1
because we started at 0.

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:30.730 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's go over it.

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:31.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:34.810 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: That would be-- the
2n minus 2 is the 2n minus

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:35.725 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:18:35.725 --> 00:18:36.350 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.610 align:middle line:84%
So it'll be-- so I'll just
write it, and then I'll explain.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:39.443 align:middle line:90%
But yeah, it's easy.

00:18:39.443 --> 00:18:41.750 align:middle line:84%
And I might get confused with
some of these fence posts

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:42.250 align:middle line:90%
issues.

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:46.690 align:middle line:84%
But I think it's going
to be Cp times 2n.

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:50.270 align:middle line:90%


00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:50.930 align:middle line:90%
Why is this?

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:53.350 align:middle line:90%
Well, we're in period n minus 1.

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:56.510 align:middle line:84%
If I accept the
offer, then I'm going

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:58.950 align:middle line:84%
to get a settlement
of S2n and minus 1.

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.890 align:middle line:84%
But I've also had to
pay my legal fees.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.070 align:middle line:84%
I'm the plaintiff, so my
legal fees are Cp per period.

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:08.390 align:middle line:84%
And we negotiated in period
0 and period 1 and period 2

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:11.570 align:middle line:84%
and period 3, all the way
up to period 2n minus 1.

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:13.490 align:middle line:84%
You might say that's
2n minus 1 periods.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:14.850 align:middle line:90%
But remember, we started at 0.

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:22.040 align:middle line:84%
So we actually have 2n
periods of negotiation costs.

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:24.760 align:middle line:84%
And then what if I
reject the offer?

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:25.780 align:middle line:90%
What's going to happen?

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:29.000 align:middle line:90%


00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:29.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:30.813 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: J minus p hat.

00:19:30.813 --> 00:19:31.480 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.273 align:middle line:90%
And we can't forget--

00:19:33.273 --> 00:19:34.940 align:middle line:84%
I've already-- I mean,
it's a sunk cost,

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:36.190 align:middle line:90%
but then we have this as well.

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:42.400 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be J minus
Cp hat minus cp times 2n.

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:43.260 align:middle line:90%
What does this mean?

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:47.600 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I reject the offer,
we're going to go to court.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:50.360 align:middle line:84%
I get a judgment of
J, as the plaintiff.

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:52.860 align:middle line:84%
I have to additionally
pay all the court costs.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:54.780 align:middle line:84%
And then I have all
these costs from before.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:56.822 align:middle line:84%
But I think what people
are kind of jumping ahead

00:19:56.822 --> 00:19:58.140 align:middle line:90%
is these don't really matter.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:00.200 align:middle line:90%
So we can circle these.

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:03.920 align:middle line:84%
At this point, these are
what are called sunk costs.

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:06.380 align:middle line:84%
I've already paid
all of these costs.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:09.140 align:middle line:84%
So in my decision today of
whether to accept or reject,

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:11.640 align:middle line:84%
these aren't actually going to
have any bearing on my choice

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:12.940 align:middle line:90%
because I pay them either way.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:13.900 align:middle line:90%
I've already paid them.

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:15.150 align:middle line:90%
I've already paid the lawyers.

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:16.760 align:middle line:84%
The only choice
I really have is,

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:23.800 align:middle line:84%
am I going to pay this
additional trial fee, Cp hat?

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:25.120 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:29.600 align:middle line:84%
So now if we compare
these two payouts,

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:32.300 align:middle line:84%
when should the plaintiff
accept the offer?

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:36.512 align:middle line:84%
What kind of offers should
the plaintiff accept?

00:20:36.512 --> 00:20:38.220 align:middle line:84%
Well, intuitively,
they're the plaintiff.

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:39.020 align:middle line:90%
They want more money.

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:41.478 align:middle line:84%
So we always think the plaintiff
should accept-- let's just

00:20:41.478 --> 00:20:42.380 align:middle line:90%
write this down here.

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:44.380 align:middle line:84%
It's always going to take
this form that they're

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:50.560 align:middle line:84%
going to accept, if and only
if, or maybe I'll say use

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:55.480 align:middle line:84%
arrows-- if and
only if S2n minus 1

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.700 align:middle line:84%
is greater than or
equal to some threshold.

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:01.160 align:middle line:84%
Let me denote this threshold
as S2n minus 1 star.

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:04.000 align:middle line:84%
And this is just-- we know this
is generally how things are.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.787 align:middle line:84%
If you're the plaintiff, this
is how much you get paid.

00:21:06.787 --> 00:21:08.620 align:middle line:84%
Getting offered more
money is always better.

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:10.078 align:middle line:84%
So it makes sense
that you're going

00:21:10.078 --> 00:21:12.160 align:middle line:84%
to accept if the
offer is high enough,

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.520 align:middle line:84%
and you're going to reject
if the offer is too low.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:17.970 align:middle line:84%
The question is, what is
this critical threshold?

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:19.770 align:middle line:84%
What's the amount that
you have to offer me

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:24.930 align:middle line:84%
as the plaintiff for me
to accept your offer?

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:25.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:27.270 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: J minus Cp hat.

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:28.690 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:30.050 align:middle line:90%
J minus Cp hat.

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:32.347 align:middle line:90%


00:21:32.347 --> 00:21:33.930 align:middle line:84%
And you might say,
well, we have to be

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:37.200 align:middle line:84%
a little careful if the offer
is exactly equal to this.

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:37.950 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll accept.

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:38.537 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I won't.

00:21:38.537 --> 00:21:40.370 align:middle line:84%
We're going to make the
convention that when

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:41.590 align:middle line:90%
indifferent, I always accept.

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:43.382 align:middle line:84%
And this is what we
have to do to make sure

00:21:43.382 --> 00:21:44.890 align:middle line:90%
that equilibrium exists.

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:46.890 align:middle line:84%
But notice what's
interesting here.

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:48.750 align:middle line:84%
What is the plaintiff
willing to accept?

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:50.530 align:middle line:84%
They're willing to
accept some offers that

00:21:50.530 --> 00:21:52.250 align:middle line:84%
are strictly less
than the amount

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:53.930 align:middle line:90%
that they would get in court.

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:55.350 align:middle line:90%
So what does this reflect?

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.370 align:middle line:84%
This reflects the cost
of going to court.

00:21:57.370 --> 00:21:59.730 align:middle line:84%
If you say, look, I'm
not going to pay you

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:01.330 align:middle line:90%
as much as you'll get in court.

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:02.413 align:middle line:90%
I'll pay you a bit less.

00:22:02.413 --> 00:22:04.330 align:middle line:84%
But it's still worth it
for you because if you

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:07.170 align:middle line:84%
accept my lowball
offer, you get to avoid

00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:09.130 align:middle line:90%
the cost of going to court.

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:11.890 align:middle line:84%
So we can see the
higher that Cp hat

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:14.850 align:middle line:84%
is, the weaker the
bargaining position

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:17.157 align:middle line:84%
of the plaintiff,
the lower the offer

00:22:17.157 --> 00:22:19.740 align:middle line:84%
that they're going to be willing
to accept because they really

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:23.060 align:middle line:84%
don't want to go to court
and pay this enormous fee.

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:28.020 align:middle line:84%
So now we go back
one step earlier.

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:30.080 align:middle line:84%
We're still in
period 2n minus 1.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:35.600 align:middle line:84%
But now let's think about
the defendant's choice.

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:39.752 align:middle line:90%


00:22:39.752 --> 00:22:41.460 align:middle line:84%
Now the question is,
how much should they

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:46.660 align:middle line:84%
offer, anticipating
that the plaintiff will

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:50.840 align:middle line:84%
accept their offer exactly
under these conditions?

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:54.180 align:middle line:90%


00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:58.220 align:middle line:84%
Well, intuitively, they
want to make the smallest

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:00.360 align:middle line:90%
offer that will be accepted.

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:02.920 align:middle line:84%
They want to pay the least
amount that they have to.

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:06.260 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be optimal
for them to choose S2n minus 1

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:08.920 align:middle line:90%
equals S2n minus 1 star.

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:12.740 align:middle line:90%


00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:15.850 align:middle line:84%
They could offer to pay
strictly more than this.

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:18.100 align:middle line:84%
That will still be accepted,
but that's worse for them

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:21.060 align:middle line:84%
because they don't want
to pay out very much.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:23.945 align:middle line:84%
I think it's a little tricky,
though, because they might also

00:23:23.945 --> 00:23:25.320 align:middle line:84%
want their offer
to get rejected.

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:26.940 align:middle line:84%
So we still have to
be a little careful.

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:27.440 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:29.000 align:middle line:90%
What if the offer gets rejected?

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:31.415 align:middle line:90%


00:23:31.415 --> 00:23:33.540 align:middle line:84%
We're claiming that they
want to make an offer that

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.915 align:middle line:84%
will be accepted,
but we actually

00:23:34.915 --> 00:23:38.180 align:middle line:84%
have to see what happens
if it gets rejected.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:40.100 align:middle line:84%
It turns out, if
it gets rejected--

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:42.740 align:middle line:84%
and let's just
work this through--

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:51.780 align:middle line:84%
this will be accepted, and then
they will get minus S2n minus 1

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:55.880 align:middle line:90%
star minus cD 2n.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:58.608 align:middle line:90%


00:23:58.608 --> 00:24:00.900 align:middle line:84%
And what if they were to make
some different offer that

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:02.620 align:middle line:90%
were rejected?

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:05.840 align:middle line:90%
What would their payout--

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:08.530 align:middle line:90%
what would their payoff be?

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:09.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:14.600 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Minus J
[INAUDIBLE] 2n minus C hat.

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:15.350 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:24:15.350 --> 00:24:17.610 align:middle line:84%
So if they make an
offer that's rejected,

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:19.070 align:middle line:84%
then they're going
to go to court.

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:21.950 align:middle line:84%
And that means they're
going to lose J.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:37.070 align:middle line:84%
They're going to lose CD hat,
and they're going to lose CD 2n

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:39.270 align:middle line:90%
But this is clearly worse.

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:42.270 align:middle line:84%
Because if they go to court,
they have to pay more.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:44.850 align:middle line:84%
They pay J rather
than J minus Cp hat,

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:46.530 align:middle line:84%
and they have to pay
the cost of court.

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:48.870 align:middle line:90%
So this is kind of extra bad.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:54.850 align:middle line:84%
So they pay more, and
they pay the court costs.

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:59.590 align:middle line:90%


00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:03.285 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see throughout
that it's never really going

00:25:03.285 --> 00:25:04.910 align:middle line:84%
to be optimal for
people to make offers

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.190 align:middle line:90%
that are going to be rejected.

00:25:06.190 --> 00:25:09.550 align:middle line:84%
It's always just going to
make things worse for them.

00:25:09.550 --> 00:25:13.050 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, this is going to
be the best choice for CD.

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:16.450 align:middle line:90%


00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:22.790 align:middle line:84%
Now let's try to go one step
back and see what's going

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:26.830 align:middle line:90%
to happen in period 2n minus 2.

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:28.970 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll come
back over here.

00:25:28.970 --> 00:25:32.630 align:middle line:90%


00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:34.970 align:middle line:84%
And then hopefully, we'll
be able to see a pattern.

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:41.390 align:middle line:90%


00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:47.530 align:middle line:84%
So now let's go to
period 2n minus 2.

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:50.350 align:middle line:90%


00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:57.110 align:middle line:90%
And our conjecture is that the--

00:25:57.110 --> 00:26:00.230 align:middle line:84%
I have to do it right
here-- the defendant is

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:07.150 align:middle line:84%
going to accept an offer of
S2n minus 2, if and only if,

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:08.500 align:middle line:90%
S2n minus 2.

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:09.400 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:13.940 align:middle line:84%
I'm the defendant now, so I'm
willing to accept low offers.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:17.280 align:middle line:84%
The plaintiff was willing
to accept high offers.

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:21.560 align:middle line:84%
The defendant is willing
to accept low offers.

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:25.080 align:middle line:84%
So there's going to be some
threshold, S2n minus 2 star,

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:28.320 align:middle line:84%
such that the defendant will
accept if they're offered

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:30.453 align:middle line:90%
to pay a small enough amount.

00:26:30.453 --> 00:26:31.620 align:middle line:90%
Is there an issue over here?

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:31.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:33.215 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Yeah, I
have a question.

00:26:33.215 --> 00:26:34.023 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yes,

00:26:34.023 --> 00:26:35.440 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Basically,
the comparison

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:38.620 align:middle line:84%
between [INAUDIBLE] giving
an offer and being rejected.

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:42.280 align:middle line:84%
So getting an offer, they have
to pay S2m minus 1 star, which

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:44.800 align:middle line:90%
is J minus Cp versus--

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:46.655 align:middle line:90%
oh, J plus.

00:26:46.655 --> 00:26:47.280 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:49.030 align:middle line:84%
So we have to be careful
with the minuses.

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:56.140 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be
minus J plus Cp hat.

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:58.185 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So does it
depend on how Cp is?

00:26:58.185 --> 00:26:59.060 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: It doesn't.

00:26:59.060 --> 00:26:59.920 align:middle line:84%
And that's why--
and this is what

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:02.040 align:middle line:84%
I meant about it's kind
of doubly bad for them.

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:05.100 align:middle line:84%
Because if their
offer is rejected,

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:08.230 align:middle line:84%
they're going to have to pay the
full settlement, J, in court.

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:11.630 align:middle line:84%
So going the defendant will
have to pay more in court.

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:14.472 align:middle line:84%
And on top of that, they'll
have to pay their legal fees.

00:27:14.472 --> 00:27:15.930 align:middle line:84%
So the difference
between these two

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:18.510 align:middle line:84%
is actually the sum
of Cp hat and Cd hat.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:20.430 align:middle line:84%
It's not about the
relationship between them.

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:20.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:21.350 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:23.058 align:middle line:84%
So just to be clear
what the argument is.

00:27:23.058 --> 00:27:26.070 align:middle line:84%
The argument is, I could make
some offer that's rejected.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.630 align:middle line:84%
If it's rejected, the amount
that I offer doesn't matter.

00:27:28.630 --> 00:27:30.430 align:middle line:90%
All that matters, it's rejected.

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:33.450 align:middle line:84%
And we've shown that that's
worse than making this offer.

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:36.478 align:middle line:84%
The alternative is I can make
an offer that will be accepted.

00:27:36.478 --> 00:27:38.270 align:middle line:84%
But to make an offer
that will be accepted,

00:27:38.270 --> 00:27:39.972 align:middle line:84%
I'd have to offer
even more than this.

00:27:39.972 --> 00:27:41.430 align:middle line:84%
And that is certainly
worse for me.

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:44.490 align:middle line:84%
So the best thing I can do
is offer the smallest amount

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:46.110 align:middle line:90%
that will be accepted.

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:53.150 align:middle line:90%


00:27:53.150 --> 00:28:07.970 align:middle line:84%
So now what our guess is that
the structure in period 2n minus

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:09.690 align:middle line:84%
2 is going to be
something like this.

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:12.148 align:middle line:84%
The defendant is going to have
some threshold where they're

00:28:12.148 --> 00:28:14.450 align:middle line:84%
going to say, look,
I'll accept the offer if

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:16.370 align:middle line:84%
and only if it's small
enough, if and only

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:18.385 align:middle line:90%
if it's below some threshold.

00:28:18.385 --> 00:28:20.510 align:middle line:84%
And then let's think about
the plaintiff's problem.

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:22.218 align:middle line:84%
We could go through
the algebra to check.

00:28:22.218 --> 00:28:27.370 align:middle line:84%
But you can see that the
plaintiff wants the offer

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:30.490 align:middle line:84%
to be, the settlement to
be as large as possible.

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:33.010 align:middle line:84%
But if it's too large,
it won't be accepted.

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:36.010 align:middle line:84%
So they want to make the largest
demand, the largest settlement

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:37.250 align:middle line:90%
that will be accepted.

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:40.770 align:middle line:84%
And that's precisely
S2n minus 2 star.

00:28:40.770 --> 00:28:43.273 align:middle line:84%
So all we have to do
is find this number.

00:28:43.273 --> 00:28:44.690 align:middle line:84%
And that's how
these games reduce.

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:47.570 align:middle line:84%
At each stage, one side is
going to accept if and only

00:28:47.570 --> 00:28:50.430 align:middle line:84%
if the offer is above
or below some threshold.

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:52.830 align:middle line:84%
And the name of the game is
finding these thresholds.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:56.010 align:middle line:90%


00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:56.910 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:28:56.910 --> 00:29:03.140 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's try to understand
what this threshold needs to be,

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:04.580 align:middle line:90%
by let's--

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:06.020 align:middle line:90%
what's going up here.

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:07.500 align:middle line:84%
So the threshold
needs to be such

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:11.620 align:middle line:84%
that they're indifferent
between accepting this offer

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:12.822 align:middle line:90%
and rejecting it.

00:29:12.822 --> 00:29:14.280 align:middle line:84%
That has to be true
at a threshold.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:15.560 align:middle line:84%
Because if they
weren't indifferent,

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:17.500 align:middle line:84%
there's no way that
could be the threshold.

00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:26.580 align:middle line:84%
So if they accept this offer,
what are they going to get?

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:29.180 align:middle line:84%
They're the defendant,
so they have to pay it.

00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:34.740 align:middle line:84%
So they're going to get
minus S2n minus 2 star

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:38.660 align:middle line:84%
minus all the legal
fees that they have.

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:44.660 align:middle line:84%
So that's minus CD
times 2n minus 1.

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:46.120 align:middle line:90%
So now we're one period earlier.

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:47.660 align:middle line:84%
So if we accept a
settlement here,

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:50.780 align:middle line:84%
we don't have to pay legal
fees in the last period.

00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:53.200 align:middle line:84%
And this is how much we're
going to have to pay.

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:55.100 align:middle line:84%
So we have a negative
sign in front.

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:58.208 align:middle line:84%
What happens if we
reject this offer?

00:29:58.208 --> 00:30:00.500 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is where backward
induction comes into play.

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:02.260 align:middle line:84%
Because in general,
I don't know what

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:04.720 align:middle line:84%
happens, what my payoff is
from rejecting this offer,

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:06.620 align:middle line:84%
because it depends on
what's then offered

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:07.800 align:middle line:90%
to me in the next period.

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:09.500 align:middle line:84%
But because we've already
used backward induction

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:10.700 align:middle line:84%
in the next period,
we know what's

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:12.158 align:middle line:84%
going to happen in
the next period.

00:30:12.158 --> 00:30:14.860 align:middle line:84%
In the next period,
the plaintiff

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:17.700 align:middle line:90%
is going to offer exactly this.

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:19.500 align:middle line:84%
And then it's going
to be optimal for me,

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:21.500 align:middle line:90%
as the defendant, to accept it.

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:23.820 align:middle line:84%
So if I wait till
the next period,

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:28.660 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to have to pay
negative S2n minus 1 star

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:30.500 align:middle line:84%
minus-- what are
my legal fees if I

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:32.560 align:middle line:90%
accept this in the next period?

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:36.240 align:middle line:90%


00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:36.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:38.397 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
CD times 2n.

00:30:38.397 --> 00:30:39.480 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Times 2n, right?

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:41.522 align:middle line:84%
Because if I reject today
and wait till tomorrow,

00:30:41.522 --> 00:30:44.120 align:middle line:84%
I have one more
period of negotiation.

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:46.960 align:middle line:84%
So that means it's
going to be CD times 2n.

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:49.500 align:middle line:90%


00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:51.300 align:middle line:90%
And now we see the pattern.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:52.860 align:middle line:90%
What are we going to find?

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:59.150 align:middle line:84%
We're going to find
that S2n minus 2 star

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:13.390 align:middle line:90%
equals S2n minus 1 star plus CD.

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:14.170 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:25.150 align:middle line:90%


00:31:25.150 --> 00:31:35.230 align:middle line:84%
Yes, that's right, which we
can do our math over here.

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:41.810 align:middle line:84%
And this is J minus
Cp hat plus CD.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:46.270 align:middle line:90%


00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:47.130 align:middle line:90%
Why is it a plus?

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:50.310 align:middle line:84%
We could try to go
through the algebra.

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:56.280 align:middle line:84%
But can we just reason through
this, why we get a plus CD here?

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:59.280 align:middle line:84%
Why am I, as the defendant,
willing to accept

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:02.240 align:middle line:84%
a settlement that's strictly
higher than the settlement I

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:03.580 align:middle line:90%
would get tomorrow?

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:04.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:04.080 --> 00:32:07.180 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Because you're
not going to tomorrow.

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:10.180 align:middle line:84%
So you're not going to have
the CD from the next period.

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:11.440 align:middle line:90%
So you're adding it back.

00:32:11.440 --> 00:32:12.440 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:14.100 align:middle line:90%
So what am I saying?

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:16.100 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying, look, I
can wait till tomorrow,

00:32:16.100 --> 00:32:17.720 align:middle line:84%
and then I have to
pay you this much.

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:20.160 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be the outcome
of negotiation tomorrow.

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:21.940 align:middle line:84%
But if I pay you
an amount today,

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:25.100 align:middle line:84%
I save one unit of
negotiation costs.

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:27.680 align:middle line:84%
So I'm actually willing
to pay more today, I'm

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:29.320 align:middle line:84%
willing to get a
worse offer today

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:33.960 align:middle line:84%
because I'm saving the legal
costs of going tomorrow.

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:37.960 align:middle line:84%
And you can see how
these enter differently.

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:40.880 align:middle line:84%
The plaintiff was also
willing to accept a worse

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:42.760 align:middle line:90%
offer to save on legal costs.

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:45.040 align:middle line:84%
But for the plaintiff,
a worse offer

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:48.000 align:middle line:84%
is a smaller settlement because
they get paid the settlement.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.560 align:middle line:84%
For the defendant, they're
willing to accept a worse

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:51.900 align:middle line:90%
offer to save on legal fees.

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:53.823 align:middle line:84%
But for them, a worse
offer means paying more.

00:32:53.823 --> 00:32:55.740 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm the defendant,
I'm basically saying,

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:59.000 align:middle line:84%
look, I'll pay you off to save
me the money of going to court.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:02.460 align:middle line:84%
And the plaintiff is saying,
you better pay me a lot.

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:05.580 align:middle line:90%


00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:06.080 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:33:06.080 --> 00:33:07.640 align:middle line:84%
I'll accept not
paying you very much

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:11.480 align:middle line:84%
in order to avoid
going to court.

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:13.640 align:middle line:84%
I'll accept you not
paying me very much

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:16.560 align:middle line:90%
to avoid going to court.

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:20.740 align:middle line:84%
So now I think we can
keep doing this all day.

00:33:20.740 --> 00:33:22.740 align:middle line:84%
But I think we're going
to see a pattern emerge.

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:25.040 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if we can guess
what the pattern is going

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:28.220 align:middle line:90%
to be for these thresholds.

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:33.240 align:middle line:90%


00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:35.880 align:middle line:84%
So it's always easy
to get off by one.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:36.960 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:37.860 align:middle line:90%
I've done it right.

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:40.880 align:middle line:90%
Let's look at the--

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:43.120 align:middle line:90%
we started in an odd period.

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:48.280 align:middle line:84%
So we have the odd periods
and the even periods.

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:59.010 align:middle line:84%
In the odd periods, the
defendant is going to propose S.

00:33:59.010 --> 00:34:12.130 align:middle line:84%
Let's say the odd period
is 2n minus 2k minus 1.

00:34:12.130 --> 00:34:16.929 align:middle line:84%
And in the even period,
it's 2n minus 2k.

00:34:16.929 --> 00:34:22.389 align:middle line:84%
And here, we're going to have
k greater than or equal to 1.

00:34:22.389 --> 00:34:25.570 align:middle line:90%


00:34:25.570 --> 00:34:28.710 align:middle line:84%
So here, let's just
write down the formula

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:30.850 align:middle line:84%
and then check that
we have it right.

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:37.330 align:middle line:84%
I think the defendant is
going to propose J minus.

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:48.010 align:middle line:84%
Cp hat plus k minus
1 of k CD minus CP.

00:34:48.010 --> 00:35:00.290 align:middle line:84%
And here, I think the
plaintiff is going

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:08.670 align:middle line:90%
to propose one step earlier.

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:14.570 align:middle line:90%


00:35:14.570 --> 00:35:16.610 align:middle line:90%
Let's check if I did this right.

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:21.450 align:middle line:90%
So let's first try k equals 1.

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:25.090 align:middle line:84%
If k equals 1, we're
in period 2n minus 1.

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:31.090 align:middle line:84%
So we better get J minus CP hat,
And that's exactly what we get.

00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:41.170 align:middle line:84%
If k equals 1 down here,
we're in 2n minus 2,

00:35:41.170 --> 00:35:45.070 align:middle line:84%
and we better get J
minus CP hat plus CD.

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:47.630 align:middle line:90%
So think that looks correct.

00:35:47.630 --> 00:35:54.500 align:middle line:90%


00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:58.620 align:middle line:84%
And then let's just check
what's happening here--

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:03.980 align:middle line:84%
accepts if and only if,
and here the defendant

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:07.300 align:middle line:90%
accepts if and only if.

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:10.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, in these periods they're
going to accept if and only if.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:14.780 align:middle line:84%
Well, the plaintiff is the
one who receives the money,

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:17.500 align:middle line:84%
is only going to accept it
if it's large enough, if

00:36:17.500 --> 00:36:19.500 align:middle line:84%
and only if the
settlement is greater than

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:22.340 align:middle line:90%
or equal to this number here.

00:36:22.340 --> 00:36:24.340 align:middle line:84%
And the defendant is going
to accept if and only

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:27.160 align:middle line:84%
if f is less than or
equal to this number here.

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:30.940 align:middle line:90%


00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:35.180 align:middle line:84%
So the defendant is proposing
the smallest settlement amount

00:36:35.180 --> 00:36:38.420 align:middle line:84%
that will be accepted because
they like low settlement

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:40.100 align:middle line:90%
amounts.

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:43.420 align:middle line:84%
And here, the plaintiff is
proposing the largest settlement

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:46.260 align:middle line:84%
amount that will be
accepted because they like

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:48.320 align:middle line:90%
to receive high settlements.

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:52.900 align:middle line:90%


00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:56.620 align:middle line:90%
So this is the strategies.

00:36:56.620 --> 00:36:59.820 align:middle line:84%
We specified all the strategies
at every contingency.

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:02.220 align:middle line:84%
But let's now compute
what actually happens.

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:06.040 align:middle line:84%
The strategies say, in every
contingency what do people do?

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:08.870 align:middle line:84%
But what actually
happens in this big game?

00:37:08.870 --> 00:37:10.120 align:middle line:90%
We made it really complicated.

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:13.800 align:middle line:84%
But what happens is actually
going to be pretty simple.

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:18.940 align:middle line:90%


00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:24.040 align:middle line:84%
You want to see what is
actually going to happen?

00:37:24.040 --> 00:37:28.420 align:middle line:90%


00:37:28.420 --> 00:37:29.958 align:middle line:84%
So when are they
going to settle?

00:37:29.958 --> 00:37:31.000 align:middle line:90%
Are they going to settle?

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:37.900 align:middle line:90%


00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:38.680 align:middle line:90%
Do we go to trial?

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:39.160 align:middle line:90%
What happens?

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:40.243 align:middle line:90%
We have the strategy here.

00:37:40.243 --> 00:37:41.797 align:middle line:84%
We just have to
figure out what's

00:37:41.797 --> 00:37:42.880 align:middle line:90%
actually going to be done.

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:52.510 align:middle line:90%


00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:55.190 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's start in the first
period, and let's go through it.

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:59.170 align:middle line:84%
In period 0, what
is going to happen?

00:37:59.170 --> 00:38:03.310 align:middle line:90%
Well, period 0 means k equals n.

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:06.790 align:middle line:84%
So the plaintiff is going to
propose this settlement amount,

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:10.770 align:middle line:84%
J minus Cp hat plus n
minus 1 times this plus Cd.

00:38:10.770 --> 00:38:14.830 align:middle line:90%


00:38:14.830 --> 00:38:17.830 align:middle line:84%
And then the defendant's
going to accept it.

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:19.390 align:middle line:90%
So that's it.

00:38:19.390 --> 00:38:21.230 align:middle line:84%
So the outcome is
actually very simple.

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:28.670 align:middle line:84%
The outcome is we settle
in period 0 for this amount

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:29.290 align:middle line:90%
down here.

00:38:29.290 --> 00:38:50.662 align:middle line:90%


00:38:50.662 --> 00:38:51.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, this looks right.

00:38:51.620 --> 00:38:54.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm just making sure I'm
not off by one index.

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:57.580 align:middle line:84%
So can we try to
interpret this amount?

00:38:57.580 --> 00:39:00.200 align:middle line:90%


00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:02.380 align:middle line:84%
How does this
amount compare to J?

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:16.320 align:middle line:90%


00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:19.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, we see that
the settlement is

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:25.080 align:middle line:84%
going to be smaller if
Cp hat is really big.

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:27.400 align:middle line:84%
So this settlement
is going to tend

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:35.200 align:middle line:90%
to be smaller if Cp hat is big.

00:39:35.200 --> 00:39:42.360 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to tend to be
bigger if Cd minus Cp is large.

00:39:42.360 --> 00:39:53.440 align:middle line:90%


00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:55.060 align:middle line:84%
So do we have
intuition for this?

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:58.040 align:middle line:90%


00:39:58.040 --> 00:39:59.160 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:03.760 align:middle line:84%
If Cd minus Cp is large,
that means the defendant

00:40:03.760 --> 00:40:07.400 align:middle line:90%
has higher negotiation costs.

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:11.520 align:middle line:84%
And that means the defendant
is at a strategic disadvantage.

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:14.840 align:middle line:84%
The fact that they have
high negotiation costs

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:18.343 align:middle line:84%
means the other side can
drive a hard bargain.

00:40:18.343 --> 00:40:19.760 align:middle line:84%
And because of
that, the defendant

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:22.800 align:middle line:84%
is going to have to end up
paying a larger settlement.

00:40:22.800 --> 00:40:26.180 align:middle line:84%
Notice that it's not only
big if Cd minus Cp is big,

00:40:26.180 --> 00:40:28.600 align:middle line:84%
but it also depends on
how far away the trial is.

00:40:28.600 --> 00:40:31.120 align:middle line:84%
So if the trial is
really far away,

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:34.360 align:middle line:84%
then the defendant
is really in trouble

00:40:34.360 --> 00:40:37.323 align:middle line:84%
because the defendant's
higher legal fees per period

00:40:37.323 --> 00:40:39.490 align:middle line:84%
could really add up over
the many, many periods that

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:41.330 align:middle line:90%
going to trial.

00:40:41.330 --> 00:40:44.330 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if
the plaintiffs cost

00:40:44.330 --> 00:40:47.050 align:middle line:84%
of going to trial is
really, really high,

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:50.050 align:middle line:84%
then the settlement is going to
be smaller because this is going

00:40:50.050 --> 00:40:52.430 align:middle line:90%
to advantage the defendant.

00:40:52.430 --> 00:40:53.930 align:middle line:84%
They're not going
to have to pay out

00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:57.090 align:middle line:84%
as much if they can drive a
hard bargain to the plaintiff

00:40:57.090 --> 00:40:58.770 align:middle line:90%
in the last period.

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:00.250 align:middle line:90%
But here's one kind of puzzle.

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:01.790 align:middle line:90%
What happened to Cd hat?

00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:08.490 align:middle line:90%


00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:09.690 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't appear at all.

00:41:09.690 --> 00:41:15.030 align:middle line:84%
So why does it not
depend at all on Cd hat?

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:19.850 align:middle line:90%


00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:20.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:22.365 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I'd say that
the plaintiff is always

00:41:22.365 --> 00:41:24.810 align:middle line:84%
going to accept on the last
day, so the cost of trial

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:26.792 align:middle line:90%
is never realized.

00:41:26.792 --> 00:41:27.750 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: That's right.

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:31.130 align:middle line:84%
But we also never get
to the fifth period.

00:41:31.130 --> 00:41:33.998 align:middle line:84%
But the costs that
we would incur

00:41:33.998 --> 00:41:35.790 align:middle line:84%
if we get to the fifth
period also show up.

00:41:35.790 --> 00:41:37.830 align:middle line:84%
So I think that's
part of the way there.

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:38.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:41.030 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Because the defendant
is the last proposer.

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:43.990 align:middle line:84%
So they can base it on what
the plaintiff will accept.

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:44.990 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:41:44.990 --> 00:41:46.950 align:middle line:84%
So if we actually
change the timing here--

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:48.370 align:middle line:84%
and this is one issue with
these bargaining games

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:50.037 align:middle line:84%
that sometimes the
details matter a lot.

00:41:50.037 --> 00:41:53.270 align:middle line:84%
If we had an odd number
of periods before trial,

00:41:53.270 --> 00:41:54.270 align:middle line:90%
this would kind of flip.

00:41:54.270 --> 00:41:57.770 align:middle line:84%
And we would care about
Cd hat, not Cp hat.

00:41:57.770 --> 00:42:01.650 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is
that the defendant

00:42:01.650 --> 00:42:05.150 align:middle line:84%
is the one able to exploit
the plaintiff's cost of trial

00:42:05.150 --> 00:42:06.570 align:middle line:84%
because the defendant
gets to make

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:08.505 align:middle line:90%
the last offer before trial.

00:42:08.505 --> 00:42:10.130 align:middle line:84%
So because the
defendant is the one who

00:42:10.130 --> 00:42:13.050 align:middle line:84%
makes the last offer before
trial, the defendant can say,

00:42:13.050 --> 00:42:14.950 align:middle line:84%
you better give me
a really good deal.

00:42:14.950 --> 00:42:16.690 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, we're
going to have to pay

00:42:16.690 --> 00:42:18.028 align:middle line:90%
this high cost to go to trial.

00:42:18.028 --> 00:42:19.070 align:middle line:90%
It's interesting, though.

00:42:19.070 --> 00:42:21.770 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the defendant also pays
a high cost to go to trial.

00:42:21.770 --> 00:42:26.232 align:middle line:84%
But they're able to exploit the
rationality of the plaintiff

00:42:26.232 --> 00:42:27.690 align:middle line:84%
and make them an
offer that they're

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:30.010 align:middle line:84%
going to have to accept, and
therefore, take advantage

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:31.110 align:middle line:90%
of their bargaining power.

00:42:31.110 --> 00:42:34.010 align:middle line:90%


00:42:34.010 --> 00:42:36.100 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:42:36.100 --> 00:42:38.260 align:middle line:90%
So I guess a few takeaways--

00:42:38.260 --> 00:42:39.820 align:middle line:84%
this model suggests
that we shouldn't

00:42:39.820 --> 00:42:42.700 align:middle line:84%
have drawn-out negotiations,
that the settlement should

00:42:42.700 --> 00:42:43.820 align:middle line:90%
end right away.

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:47.420 align:middle line:84%
But notice that the potential
length of the negotiation

00:42:47.420 --> 00:42:48.640 align:middle line:90%
affects the outcome.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:50.980 align:middle line:84%
So even though we settle
in the very first period,

00:42:50.980 --> 00:42:54.420 align:middle line:84%
the potential to have a very
long, drawn-out negotiation

00:42:54.420 --> 00:42:58.033 align:middle line:84%
drives the amount that we
settle for in this first period.

00:42:58.033 --> 00:42:58.700 align:middle line:90%
One final thing.

00:42:58.700 --> 00:43:00.860 align:middle line:84%
If on an exam you're
asked about this,

00:43:00.860 --> 00:43:03.640 align:middle line:84%
the strategy is not we
settle in the first period.

00:43:03.640 --> 00:43:05.720 align:middle line:84%
The strategy is this
really complicated thing.

00:43:05.720 --> 00:43:08.460 align:middle line:84%
The outcome is that we
settle in the first period.

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:11.920 align:middle line:84%
Now, in reality, we often see
long drawn-out negotiations.

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:14.380 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's often very
helpful in economic models

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:15.780 align:middle line:90%
to say, what's missing?

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:17.680 align:middle line:84%
Why is it that in
reality-- let's

00:43:17.680 --> 00:43:19.180 align:middle line:84%
say Trump and the
Democrats, I think

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:21.680 align:middle line:84%
they're going to be negotiating
for a pretty long time would

00:43:21.680 --> 00:43:23.220 align:middle line:90%
be my guess.

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:25.220 align:middle line:84%
That's completely consistent
with the prediction

00:43:25.220 --> 00:43:26.040 align:middle line:90%
of this model.

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.580 align:middle line:84%
So what do you think is
missing in this model that

00:43:28.580 --> 00:43:30.800 align:middle line:84%
explains why everything
ends in the first period?

00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:31.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:43:31.540 --> 00:43:33.480 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Uncertainty
about what J actually is.

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:34.230 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:36.578 align:middle line:84%
An uncertainty about the
preferences of the other side,

00:43:36.578 --> 00:43:37.870 align:middle line:90%
what they're willing to accept.

00:43:37.870 --> 00:43:39.990 align:middle line:84%
Here, we all know exactly
what the other side

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:42.670 align:middle line:84%
is going to accept, and
we can push them just

00:43:42.670 --> 00:43:44.643 align:middle line:84%
to the brink of
acceptance at every round.

00:43:44.643 --> 00:43:47.310 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, there's no reason
for things to evolve over time.

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:51.070 align:middle line:84%
The reason that we often have
delay and impasses in bargaining

00:43:51.070 --> 00:43:55.150 align:middle line:84%
is that we don't know what the
other side is willing to accept,

00:43:55.150 --> 00:43:57.150 align:middle line:84%
and we might want to make
them a really bad deal

00:43:57.150 --> 00:44:01.550 align:middle line:84%
early on on the off chance
that they end up not

00:44:01.550 --> 00:44:03.100 align:middle line:90%
being who we thought they were.

00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:05.350 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see later in the
course models of bargaining

00:44:05.350 --> 00:44:07.230 align:middle line:84%
with incomplete
information or uncertainty

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:08.607 align:middle line:84%
about the opponent's
preferences.

00:44:08.607 --> 00:44:10.690 align:middle line:84%
And that will exactly lead
to delay in bargaining.

00:44:10.690 --> 00:44:12.440 align:middle line:84%
Imagine you were
negotiating with someone,

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:14.565 align:middle line:84%
and you didn't know if they
were a tough negotiator

00:44:14.565 --> 00:44:15.470 align:middle line:90%
or a soft negotiator.

00:44:15.470 --> 00:44:17.470 align:middle line:84%
You might want to make a
really ridiculous offer

00:44:17.470 --> 00:44:19.762 align:middle line:84%
in the first period that only
the soft negotiator would

00:44:19.762 --> 00:44:22.053 align:middle line:84%
accept, but that you know
the hard negotiator would not

00:44:22.053 --> 00:44:22.590 align:middle line:90%
accept.

00:44:22.590 --> 00:44:25.007 align:middle line:84%
And then move to the next
period and change your strategy.

00:44:25.007 --> 00:44:27.245 align:middle line:84%
So that's the basic
idea of what goes

00:44:27.245 --> 00:44:28.870 align:middle line:84%
on when you have
incomplete information

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:30.590 align:middle line:90%
about the other players.

00:44:30.590 --> 00:44:36.630 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's now move to the
second bargaining situation

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:39.650 align:middle line:84%
today, which is going to
be haggling over the price.

00:44:39.650 --> 00:44:47.590 align:middle line:90%


00:44:47.590 --> 00:44:50.640 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going
to imagine that--

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:52.170 align:middle line:90%
we'll call this price haggling.

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:56.590 align:middle line:90%


00:44:56.590 --> 00:45:02.590 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose that we
have a seller and a buyer.

00:45:02.590 --> 00:45:10.430 align:middle line:84%
And the seller just
wants to get paid.

00:45:10.430 --> 00:45:13.110 align:middle line:84%
They're negotiating over
the price of some item.

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:16.550 align:middle line:84%
So if they trade at
price p, then the seller,

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:19.510 align:middle line:84%
their utility is going to be
the price p that they get.

00:45:19.510 --> 00:45:22.830 align:middle line:84%
The buyer's utility is
going to be v minus p.

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:27.630 align:middle line:84%
So that means p is
the price, and v is

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:29.090 align:middle line:90%
the buyer's value for the good.

00:45:29.090 --> 00:45:33.360 align:middle line:90%


00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:36.280 align:middle line:84%
So notice here, we're assuming
that it doesn't cost anything

00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:38.060 align:middle line:84%
for the seller to
produce the good.

00:45:38.060 --> 00:45:39.393 align:middle line:90%
That's a simplifying assumption.

00:45:39.393 --> 00:45:40.518 align:middle line:90%
It's already been produced.

00:45:40.518 --> 00:45:42.300 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they can't resell
it to anyone else.

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:43.580 align:middle line:90%
They're just stuck with it.

00:45:43.580 --> 00:45:46.160 align:middle line:84%
So the seller values
the good at 0.

00:45:46.160 --> 00:45:50.600 align:middle line:84%
They're thinking of selling it
to the buyer who values it at v,

00:45:50.600 --> 00:45:53.240 align:middle line:84%
and they're trying to haggle
over what the price should be.

00:45:53.240 --> 00:45:55.000 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, let's
just make sure we

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:58.540 align:middle line:84%
understand what's a reasonable
range of what the price can be?

00:45:58.540 --> 00:46:00.940 align:middle line:84%
So I think there's some
price that we can rule out.

00:46:00.940 --> 00:46:04.240 align:middle line:90%
How low could the price be?

00:46:04.240 --> 00:46:06.515 align:middle line:84%
What's the lowest it
could possibly be?

00:46:06.515 --> 00:46:08.140 align:middle line:84%
Well, actually, it
could be pretty low.

00:46:08.140 --> 00:46:10.473 align:middle line:84%
I think it could go all the
way down to 0, in principle.

00:46:10.473 --> 00:46:13.280 align:middle line:84%
It's certainly not going
to be less than 0, though.

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:15.520 align:middle line:84%
Because if it was
less than 0, well,

00:46:15.520 --> 00:46:18.800 align:middle line:84%
the seller would never accept
being paid a negative price.

00:46:18.800 --> 00:46:20.520 align:middle line:84%
What about the
highest price that we

00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:23.780 align:middle line:90%
would expect to see here?

00:46:23.780 --> 00:46:24.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:24.280 --> 00:46:24.960 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: v.

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:26.877 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: v. It certainly
can't be more than v

00:46:26.877 --> 00:46:31.640 align:middle line:84%
because the buyer would
never accept a price above v.

00:46:31.640 --> 00:46:38.280 align:middle line:84%
So we can, at first pass, just
by basic individual rationality

00:46:38.280 --> 00:46:40.680 align:middle line:84%
concerns, see that the price
is going to be somewhere

00:46:40.680 --> 00:46:42.480 align:middle line:84%
between 0 and v,
and both players

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:44.520 align:middle line:84%
are going to get some
non-negative utility.

00:46:44.520 --> 00:46:47.160 align:middle line:84%
But the question is, where
is it between 0 and v?

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:50.000 align:middle line:84%
The seller would love it
to be very close to v,

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:52.360 align:middle line:84%
and basically leave
the buyer with nothing

00:46:52.360 --> 00:46:54.560 align:middle line:84%
and charge them their full
valuation for the good.

00:46:54.560 --> 00:46:57.133 align:middle line:84%
The buyer would love
to pay a price that's

00:46:57.133 --> 00:46:58.800 align:middle line:84%
really, really low,
so that they can get

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:00.240 align:middle line:90%
all the value from the good.

00:47:00.240 --> 00:47:02.720 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to try to make
some predictions about where

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:05.880 align:middle line:84%
within this interval
the price can be.

00:47:05.880 --> 00:47:08.000 align:middle line:84%
Notice this is kind
of a common situation

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:11.440 align:middle line:84%
that we generally
only expect that goods

00:47:11.440 --> 00:47:13.720 align:middle line:84%
are going to be exchanged
when the seller values

00:47:13.720 --> 00:47:15.640 align:middle line:90%
the good more than the buyer.

00:47:15.640 --> 00:47:18.820 align:middle line:84%
And we generally expect the
price to be somewhere between--

00:47:18.820 --> 00:47:21.320 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- when the seller values
the good less than the buyer.

00:47:21.320 --> 00:47:22.945 align:middle line:84%
And we expect the
price to be somewhere

00:47:22.945 --> 00:47:24.720 align:middle line:84%
between what the
seller values it at

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:26.370 align:middle line:90%
and what the buyer values it at.

00:47:26.370 --> 00:47:29.290 align:middle line:84%
And here, what the
seller values it at is 0.

00:47:29.290 --> 00:47:33.450 align:middle line:84%
You can think of this
as the seller's value,

00:47:33.450 --> 00:47:36.810 align:middle line:90%
and this is the buyer's value.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:38.435 align:middle line:90%
So the buyer does value it more.

00:47:38.435 --> 00:47:40.310 align:middle line:84%
That's why it makes
sense for them to buy it.

00:47:40.310 --> 00:47:43.330 align:middle line:84%
But where the price lies
between these two valuations

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:44.350 align:middle line:90%
is the name of the game.

00:47:44.350 --> 00:47:46.668 align:middle line:84%
That's what we're
trying to figure out.

00:47:46.668 --> 00:47:48.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, what we're also
going to imagine

00:47:48.210 --> 00:47:52.210 align:middle line:90%
is that there's a cost of delay.

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:54.770 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to model the
cost of delay a bit differently.

00:47:54.770 --> 00:47:57.490 align:middle line:84%
Here, we model the cost of
delay as an additive cost.

00:47:57.490 --> 00:48:02.130 align:middle line:84%
Each period we had to pay
a lawyer some daily fee,

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:03.610 align:middle line:90%
and that had a cost.

00:48:03.610 --> 00:48:05.490 align:middle line:84%
Here, we're going to
say the cost of delay,

00:48:05.490 --> 00:48:08.750 align:middle line:84%
we're going to use what's
called discounting.

00:48:08.750 --> 00:48:11.930 align:middle line:90%


00:48:11.930 --> 00:48:20.570 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is that if
we trade in period t,

00:48:20.570 --> 00:48:22.970 align:middle line:84%
then the utilities
we actually get

00:48:22.970 --> 00:48:32.300 align:middle line:84%
would be delta p for the
seller, and delta v minus p

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:41.600 align:middle line:84%
for the buyer to the t, where
delta is between 0 and 1.

00:48:41.600 --> 00:48:44.260 align:middle line:90%


00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:46.760 align:middle line:84%
So here-- you may have seen
this in other economics courses.

00:48:46.760 --> 00:48:49.540 align:middle line:84%
Delta is called our
discount factor.

00:48:49.540 --> 00:48:51.780 align:middle line:84%
And what this says
is each period

00:48:51.780 --> 00:48:55.540 align:middle line:84%
we wait our utilities get
multiplied by this number

00:48:55.540 --> 00:48:56.800 align:middle line:90%
delta, which is less than 1.

00:48:56.800 --> 00:48:59.140 align:middle line:90%
And therefore, they get smaller.

00:48:59.140 --> 00:49:02.820 align:middle line:84%
So if t is really, really
big, then the seller

00:49:02.820 --> 00:49:05.000 align:middle line:84%
doesn't get the full
utility from the price.

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:07.080 align:middle line:84%
They get that times
delta to the t

00:49:07.080 --> 00:49:08.660 align:middle line:84%
because each period
they have to wait

00:49:08.660 --> 00:49:10.500 align:middle line:84%
beyond the zeroth
period they incur

00:49:10.500 --> 00:49:13.140 align:middle line:84%
this multiplicative
discount of delta.

00:49:13.140 --> 00:49:14.360 align:middle line:90%
And similarly, for the buyer.

00:49:14.360 --> 00:49:17.740 align:middle line:84%
They incur this multiplicative
discount of delta.

00:49:17.740 --> 00:49:22.860 align:middle line:84%
So let's understand if
delta is very close to 1,

00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:27.420 align:middle line:84%
then that would indicate that
the players are quite patient.

00:49:27.420 --> 00:49:30.220 align:middle line:84%
Because as time
passes, delta to the t

00:49:30.220 --> 00:49:34.020 align:middle line:84%
doesn't get smaller that quickly
if delta is very close to 1.

00:49:34.020 --> 00:49:37.860 align:middle line:84%
If delta is very close to 0, the
players are extremely impatient.

00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:41.980 align:middle line:84%
And as time passes, their
utility is going to drop a lot.

00:49:41.980 --> 00:49:44.340 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the cost of
delay becomes very high.

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:49.820 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll-- you can
say this in a few ways.

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:54.220 align:middle line:84%
You might say low delta
is another way of saying

00:49:54.220 --> 00:49:57.340 align:middle line:90%
the players are impatient.

00:49:57.340 --> 00:49:59.840 align:middle line:84%
Or another way of saying that
is the cost of delay is high.

00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:04.220 align:middle line:90%


00:50:04.220 --> 00:50:06.620 align:middle line:84%
And then conversely,
if we have high delta,

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:09.340 align:middle line:84%
the players are very patient,
and the cost of delay

00:50:09.340 --> 00:50:10.320 align:middle line:90%
is not very high.

00:50:10.320 --> 00:50:14.900 align:middle line:90%


00:50:14.900 --> 00:50:16.460 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes we think
of delta as being

00:50:16.460 --> 00:50:17.668 align:middle line:90%
related to the interest rate.

00:50:17.668 --> 00:50:20.372 align:middle line:84%
So in a financial context,
the cost of delay with money

00:50:20.372 --> 00:50:21.830 align:middle line:84%
is the interest
rate that you could

00:50:21.830 --> 00:50:24.310 align:middle line:84%
have gotten by putting that
money in a bank account

00:50:24.310 --> 00:50:26.410 align:middle line:84%
or getting some
return in equities.

00:50:26.410 --> 00:50:29.270 align:middle line:90%


00:50:29.270 --> 00:50:34.990 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going to assume
that time proceeds just as

00:50:34.990 --> 00:50:35.490 align:middle line:90%
before.

00:50:35.490 --> 00:50:44.878 align:middle line:90%


00:50:44.878 --> 00:50:46.670 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is kind
of exactly what you

00:50:46.670 --> 00:50:48.087 align:middle line:84%
see if you go a
market, especially

00:50:48.087 --> 00:50:50.510 align:middle line:84%
in developing countries and
there aren't posted prices.

00:50:50.510 --> 00:50:51.770 align:middle line:84%
You, as the buyer,
go up and you say,

00:50:51.770 --> 00:50:52.690 align:middle line:90%
I'm willing to pay you this.

00:50:52.690 --> 00:50:53.570 align:middle line:90%
And they say, oh, no.

00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:54.490 align:middle line:90%
You need to pay me this much.

00:50:54.490 --> 00:50:56.698 align:middle line:84%
And then you counteroffer,
and you go back and forth.

00:50:56.698 --> 00:50:59.670 align:middle line:84%
And even in developed
countries, this certainly

00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:01.950 align:middle line:84%
happens if we're negotiating
on some big deal.

00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:03.370 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't happen at Starbucks.

00:51:03.370 --> 00:51:04.870 align:middle line:84%
You're probably not going
to be able to negotiate over

00:51:04.870 --> 00:51:05.912 align:middle line:90%
the price of your coffee.

00:51:05.912 --> 00:51:07.590 align:middle line:84%
But if you're getting
hired at a firm,

00:51:07.590 --> 00:51:09.230 align:middle line:84%
or if you're making
a merger, people

00:51:09.230 --> 00:51:13.030 align:middle line:84%
do negotiate all
the time on prices.

00:51:13.030 --> 00:51:18.550 align:middle line:84%
So 2n minus 2, 2n
minus 1, and then 2n.

00:51:18.550 --> 00:51:21.190 align:middle line:84%
And the order I think
we're going to assume

00:51:21.190 --> 00:51:26.350 align:middle line:84%
is the seller makes
the first offer.

00:51:26.350 --> 00:51:30.958 align:middle line:84%
So here, the seller
offers a price, P0.

00:51:30.958 --> 00:51:32.750 align:middle line:84%
So instead of offering
a settlement amount,

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:34.070 align:middle line:90%
they're offering a price.

00:51:34.070 --> 00:51:37.650 align:middle line:84%
The buyer can either
accept or reject.

00:51:37.650 --> 00:51:42.550 align:middle line:90%


00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:44.010 align:middle line:90%
If it's accepted, we're done.

00:51:44.010 --> 00:51:45.510 align:middle line:84%
If it's rejected,
now the buyer gets

00:51:45.510 --> 00:51:48.710 align:middle line:84%
to make a counteroffer
of P1, and the seller

00:51:48.710 --> 00:51:50.630 align:middle line:90%
can accept or reject.

00:51:50.630 --> 00:51:55.070 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to go on like
this, all the way up to seller

00:51:55.070 --> 00:51:57.950 align:middle line:90%
making an offer P2n minus 2.

00:51:57.950 --> 00:52:00.990 align:middle line:84%
Buyer making an
offer P2n minus 1.

00:52:00.990 --> 00:52:04.570 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to assume
at period 2n, the world ends.

00:52:04.570 --> 00:52:05.410 align:middle line:90%
Or just, that's it.

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:06.630 align:middle line:90%
We both get 0.

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:14.590 align:middle line:84%
So if we wait this long,
we get payoffs of 0, 0.

00:52:14.590 --> 00:52:16.720 align:middle line:84%
This seems like a
strange assumption.

00:52:16.720 --> 00:52:18.417 align:middle line:84%
The real reason is
that we don't quite

00:52:18.417 --> 00:52:20.000 align:middle line:84%
have the tools to
analyze what happens

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:21.880 align:middle line:90%
if it could go on indefinitely.

00:52:21.880 --> 00:52:24.902 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that if you
take n to be really, really big,

00:52:24.902 --> 00:52:26.360 align:middle line:84%
the predictions
that we'll get will

00:52:26.360 --> 00:52:27.777 align:middle line:84%
be very similar
to the predictions

00:52:27.777 --> 00:52:29.380 align:middle line:84%
if there's an
indefinite end date.

00:52:29.380 --> 00:52:32.320 align:middle line:84%
So as I tell the story of the
tourists get off the tour bus

00:52:32.320 --> 00:52:34.852 align:middle line:84%
at the market, and the tour
bus is leaving in 2n periods,

00:52:34.852 --> 00:52:37.060 align:middle line:84%
and this is the last chance
for them to make a trade.

00:52:37.060 --> 00:52:40.260 align:middle line:84%
But are they really-- or people
say the ice cream cone melts.

00:52:40.260 --> 00:52:43.040 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, I don't know how
compelling this exact end

00:52:43.040 --> 00:52:45.960 align:middle line:84%
date is, and we'll talk about
relaxing that assumption later

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:46.460 align:middle line:90%
on.

00:52:46.460 --> 00:52:49.900 align:middle line:90%


00:52:49.900 --> 00:52:56.780 align:middle line:84%
So now let's try to
start analyzing this.

00:52:56.780 --> 00:53:12.240 align:middle line:90%


00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:20.770 align:middle line:84%
So let's start, as usual,
in period 2n minus 1.

00:53:20.770 --> 00:53:25.590 align:middle line:84%
And now what is the
seller going to accept?

00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:28.410 align:middle line:90%


00:53:28.410 --> 00:53:30.630 align:middle line:90%
So we're in period 2n minus 1.

00:53:30.630 --> 00:53:33.170 align:middle line:84%
The world is going
to end tomorrow.

00:53:33.170 --> 00:53:34.590 align:middle line:90%
The buyer makes an offer.

00:53:34.590 --> 00:53:38.810 align:middle line:84%
What prices will the seller
be willing to accept?

00:53:38.810 --> 00:53:43.410 align:middle line:84%
So they'll accept if and only if
the offered price, P2n minus 1,

00:53:43.410 --> 00:53:45.230 align:middle line:84%
is greater than
or equal to what?

00:53:45.230 --> 00:53:54.330 align:middle line:90%


00:53:54.330 --> 00:53:55.110 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts?

00:53:55.110 --> 00:53:58.010 align:middle line:90%


00:53:58.010 --> 00:53:59.150 align:middle line:90%
So you're the seller.

00:53:59.150 --> 00:54:01.510 align:middle line:84%
The buyer says, I'm going to
pay you this amount for it.

00:54:01.510 --> 00:54:03.177 align:middle line:84%
You say, well, either
I can accept this,

00:54:03.177 --> 00:54:05.450 align:middle line:84%
or I can reject it
and get a payoff of 0.

00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:07.110 align:middle line:90%
So what am I willing to accept?

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:07.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:54:07.610 --> 00:54:09.010 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Won't it just be 0?

00:54:09.010 --> 00:54:11.650 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah,
anything that's above 0.

00:54:11.650 --> 00:54:13.350 align:middle line:90%
Exactly 0, we can argue about.

00:54:13.350 --> 00:54:16.370 align:middle line:84%
But we'll say if and only if
it's greater than or equal to 0.

00:54:16.370 --> 00:54:18.970 align:middle line:84%
So if you offer me any
non-negative price,

00:54:18.970 --> 00:54:22.850 align:middle line:84%
my choice is I accept it, or I
wait till tomorrow and I get 0.

00:54:22.850 --> 00:54:24.650 align:middle line:84%
So any positive amount
you give me, I'm

00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:27.250 align:middle line:84%
willing to accept because it's
better than getting 0 tomorrow.

00:54:27.250 --> 00:54:29.610 align:middle line:84%
And given that, what is
the buyer going to offer?

00:54:29.610 --> 00:54:31.330 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're
working backwards.

00:54:31.330 --> 00:54:33.377 align:middle line:90%
What will the buyer offer?

00:54:33.377 --> 00:54:34.710 align:middle line:90%
How much will they offer to pay?

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:40.410 align:middle line:90%


00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:42.417 align:middle line:84%
It's a good position
for the buyer, right?

00:54:42.417 --> 00:54:43.750 align:middle line:90%
They're going to offer to pay 0.

00:54:43.750 --> 00:54:47.370 align:middle line:90%


00:54:47.370 --> 00:54:50.130 align:middle line:84%
If I know that the seller
will accept any price,

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:54.090 align:middle line:84%
I'll offer the smallest
price they'll accept.

00:54:54.090 --> 00:54:56.810 align:middle line:90%
What's the story here?

00:54:56.810 --> 00:54:58.975 align:middle line:84%
I come at the end of
the day, think about--

00:54:58.975 --> 00:55:00.350 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we see
this all the time.

00:55:00.350 --> 00:55:00.892 align:middle line:90%
It's a store.

00:55:00.892 --> 00:55:03.190 align:middle line:90%
They have one croissant left.

00:55:03.190 --> 00:55:04.270 align:middle line:90%
They're about to close.

00:55:04.270 --> 00:55:06.210 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe they have tons
of croissants left.

00:55:06.210 --> 00:55:07.725 align:middle line:84%
I know if they
don't sell it to me,

00:55:07.725 --> 00:55:09.350 align:middle line:84%
they're not going to
sell it to anyone.

00:55:09.350 --> 00:55:10.520 align:middle line:90%
It'll go stale tomorrow.

00:55:10.520 --> 00:55:11.700 align:middle line:90%
I can make a pretty good--

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:13.200 align:middle line:84%
I'm in a pretty strong
bargaining position.

00:55:13.200 --> 00:55:14.880 align:middle line:84%
I can say, just give it
to me for a price of 0.

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:16.297 align:middle line:84%
And we see bakeries
have discounts

00:55:16.297 --> 00:55:19.220 align:middle line:84%
at the end of the day
for exactly this reason.

00:55:19.220 --> 00:55:22.660 align:middle line:84%
But now let's go to
period 2n minus 2.

00:55:22.660 --> 00:55:27.260 align:middle line:84%
Now the buyer is
making an offer.

00:55:27.260 --> 00:55:29.060 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:55:29.060 --> 00:55:30.380 align:middle line:90%
The seller is making an offer.

00:55:30.380 --> 00:55:32.880 align:middle line:84%
So the buyer is choosing whether
to accept the offer or not.

00:55:32.880 --> 00:55:36.020 align:middle line:90%


00:55:36.020 --> 00:55:38.420 align:middle line:84%
And what offers will
the buyer accept?

00:55:38.420 --> 00:55:45.180 align:middle line:84%
So the buyer will accept if the
price, pt, offered by the seller

00:55:45.180 --> 00:55:48.620 align:middle line:90%
is small enough.

00:55:48.620 --> 00:55:49.940 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, question or-- yeah?

00:55:49.940 --> 00:55:52.573 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is the buyer's value
of the good known to the seller?

00:55:52.573 --> 00:55:53.240 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: It is.

00:55:53.240 --> 00:55:54.573 align:middle line:90%
And that's a crucial assumption.

00:55:54.573 --> 00:55:56.140 align:middle line:90%
So it is known.

00:55:56.140 --> 00:55:58.463 align:middle line:84%
And in reality, you might
think it wouldn't be known.

00:55:58.463 --> 00:55:59.880 align:middle line:84%
And towards the
end of the course,

00:55:59.880 --> 00:56:01.520 align:middle line:84%
we'll talk about incomplete
information bargaining.

00:56:01.520 --> 00:56:03.080 align:middle line:90%
But it comes really messy.

00:56:03.080 --> 00:56:04.447 align:middle line:90%
But yes, v is known.

00:56:04.447 --> 00:56:05.280 align:middle line:90%
That's a good point.

00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:06.560 align:middle line:90%
And delta is also known.

00:56:06.560 --> 00:56:11.060 align:middle line:90%


00:56:11.060 --> 00:56:15.220 align:middle line:84%
So here, we're going
to have, say, Pt star.

00:56:15.220 --> 00:56:20.020 align:middle line:84%
The buyer will accept if
the price is low enough.

00:56:20.020 --> 00:56:25.300 align:middle line:84%
And let's try to compute
what Pt star has to be.

00:56:25.300 --> 00:56:28.420 align:middle line:84%
So as usual, Pt star is going
to be defined by an indifference

00:56:28.420 --> 00:56:29.300 align:middle line:90%
condition.

00:56:29.300 --> 00:56:31.540 align:middle line:84%
The highest price they'll
accept is the price

00:56:31.540 --> 00:56:34.180 align:middle line:84%
where if they accept
it, they do just as well

00:56:34.180 --> 00:56:37.540 align:middle line:84%
as if they were to reject
it and wait until tomorrow.

00:56:37.540 --> 00:56:42.660 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at what happens
if they were to accept a price

00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:50.940 align:middle line:84%
of Pt star, then they're going
to get delta to the 2n minus 2

00:56:50.940 --> 00:56:54.360 align:middle line:90%
of v minus Pt star.

00:56:54.360 --> 00:57:00.900 align:middle line:90%


00:57:00.900 --> 00:57:02.160 align:middle line:90%
So that's if they accept.

00:57:02.160 --> 00:57:06.190 align:middle line:90%


00:57:06.190 --> 00:57:07.610 align:middle line:90%
What if they reject the offer?

00:57:07.610 --> 00:57:11.550 align:middle line:90%


00:57:11.550 --> 00:57:16.122 align:middle line:84%
If they reject the offer, then
tomorrow they get to propose,

00:57:16.122 --> 00:57:17.330 align:middle line:90%
and they're in a great shape.

00:57:17.330 --> 00:57:21.350 align:middle line:84%
We already said tomorrow they're
going to offer a price of 0,

00:57:21.350 --> 00:57:23.030 align:middle line:90%
and it's going to be accepted.

00:57:23.030 --> 00:57:26.590 align:middle line:84%
So that means tomorrow they're
going to get delta to the 2n

00:57:26.590 --> 00:57:34.590 align:middle line:84%
minus 1 times v. Really,
it's minus v PT plus 1 star.

00:57:34.590 --> 00:57:39.130 align:middle line:84%
Or I guess I should say,
here, t equals 2n minus 2.

00:57:39.130 --> 00:57:43.470 align:middle line:84%
But I'm just writing t
because it's a bit cleaner.

00:57:43.470 --> 00:57:47.270 align:middle line:84%
And once again, we can see that
we can simplify these discount

00:57:47.270 --> 00:57:48.430 align:middle line:90%
factors a lot.

00:57:48.430 --> 00:57:51.950 align:middle line:84%
Because we can factor out delta
to the 2n minus 1 from both

00:57:51.950 --> 00:57:52.490 align:middle line:90%
terms.

00:57:52.490 --> 00:57:55.590 align:middle line:84%
And this makes sense because the
question is, do I take it today,

00:57:55.590 --> 00:57:56.882 align:middle line:90%
or do I wait till tomorrow?

00:57:56.882 --> 00:57:59.090 align:middle line:84%
There's really only one
discount factor that matters.

00:57:59.090 --> 00:58:01.090 align:middle line:84%
It's the discounting
between today and tomorrow.

00:58:01.090 --> 00:58:03.510 align:middle line:84%
So we can equivalently--
we can cancel things out,

00:58:03.510 --> 00:58:06.720 align:middle line:90%
and we can say--

00:58:06.720 --> 00:58:08.960 align:middle line:90%
and just put a delta here.

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:11.980 align:middle line:84%
So we divide by delta
to the 2n minus 2.

00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:14.760 align:middle line:90%


00:58:14.760 --> 00:58:21.160 align:middle line:84%
So what we get is v. And maybe
I'll write the general formula--

00:58:21.160 --> 00:58:25.540 align:middle line:90%
v minus Pt star equals delta.

00:58:25.540 --> 00:58:36.600 align:middle line:90%


00:58:36.600 --> 00:58:41.400 align:middle line:84%
In period t, in an even
period where I'm the buyer,

00:58:41.400 --> 00:58:44.640 align:middle line:84%
I can either get
this utility today,

00:58:44.640 --> 00:58:47.025 align:middle line:84%
or I can wait till tomorrow
and get this utility

00:58:47.025 --> 00:58:48.400 align:middle line:84%
where the price
I get tomorrow is

00:58:48.400 --> 00:58:50.858 align:middle line:84%
going to depend on what we've
already computed for tomorrow

00:58:50.858 --> 00:58:51.980 align:middle line:90%
using backward induction.

00:58:51.980 --> 00:58:55.680 align:middle line:90%


00:58:55.680 --> 00:58:59.620 align:middle line:84%
And if we try to solve
for this, we'll get Pt.

00:58:59.620 --> 00:59:19.000 align:middle line:90%


00:59:19.000 --> 00:59:23.680 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's ask, how does this
Pt star, this threshold price,

00:59:23.680 --> 00:59:25.460 align:middle line:90%
compare to Pt plus 1?

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:33.480 align:middle line:90%


00:59:33.480 --> 00:59:35.940 align:middle line:84%
Is it bigger or
smaller than Pt plus 1?

00:59:35.940 --> 00:59:44.080 align:middle line:90%


00:59:44.080 --> 00:59:49.760 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's bigger because Pt
is a weighted average of Pt

00:59:49.760 --> 00:59:52.480 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 and the
number v. But we know

00:59:52.480 --> 00:59:55.120 align:middle line:84%
v is always bigger than
or equal to Pt plus 1.

00:59:55.120 --> 00:59:57.960 align:middle line:84%
So that means this is strictly
greater than Pt plus 1.

00:59:57.960 --> 01:00:00.970 align:middle line:84%
And let's try to think through
the intuition for this.

01:00:00.970 --> 01:00:03.450 align:middle line:90%
Again, it's about discounting.

01:00:03.450 --> 01:00:07.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm willing to accept a
price that's higher today

01:00:07.290 --> 01:00:09.810 align:middle line:84%
than I would get tomorrow
for the classic reason

01:00:09.810 --> 01:00:13.610 align:middle line:84%
that if I wait till tomorrow,
I'd have to pay a cost of delay.

01:00:13.610 --> 01:00:15.970 align:middle line:84%
So I'm willing to accept
a worse deal today

01:00:15.970 --> 01:00:17.530 align:middle line:84%
than I would get
tomorrow in order

01:00:17.530 --> 01:00:19.170 align:middle line:90%
to avoid the cost of delay.

01:00:19.170 --> 01:00:23.570 align:middle line:84%
For me, a worse deal is a higher
price because I'm the buyer.

01:00:23.570 --> 01:00:26.010 align:middle line:84%
So I started out with
a case of 2n minus 2,

01:00:26.010 --> 01:00:28.890 align:middle line:84%
but you can see that this
formula actually holds for any

01:00:28.890 --> 01:00:29.870 align:middle line:90%
even period.

01:00:29.870 --> 01:00:33.130 align:middle line:90%


01:00:33.130 --> 01:00:35.690 align:middle line:84%
For any even period
t, the buyer is

01:00:35.690 --> 01:00:38.330 align:middle line:84%
going to accept if the
price is below a threshold.

01:00:38.330 --> 01:00:43.490 align:middle line:84%
The seller is then going to
offer exactly that threshold.

01:00:43.490 --> 01:00:46.710 align:middle line:84%
And the threshold is
defined by this formula.

01:00:46.710 --> 01:00:52.650 align:middle line:90%


01:00:52.650 --> 01:00:56.850 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's try to
look at odd periods t.

01:00:56.850 --> 01:01:00.530 align:middle line:84%
If we're in an odd
period t, now it

01:01:00.530 --> 01:01:05.530 align:middle line:84%
is the seller who's deciding
whether to accept or reject.

01:01:05.530 --> 01:01:11.370 align:middle line:84%
The seller is going to
accept if Pt is greater

01:01:11.370 --> 01:01:15.810 align:middle line:90%
than or equal to Pt star.

01:01:15.810 --> 01:01:19.890 align:middle line:84%
And now can we get a formula
for what Pt star is going to be?

01:01:19.890 --> 01:01:23.850 align:middle line:84%
It has to say that the seller
is indifferent between selling

01:01:23.850 --> 01:01:28.130 align:middle line:84%
at a price of Pt star today
or waiting until tomorrow.

01:01:28.130 --> 01:01:32.010 align:middle line:84%
If they sell at Pt
star today, they

01:01:32.010 --> 01:01:35.110 align:middle line:90%
get Pt star in discounted terms.

01:01:35.110 --> 01:01:36.790 align:middle line:84%
What happens if they
wait till tomorrow?

01:01:36.790 --> 01:01:43.290 align:middle line:90%


01:01:43.290 --> 01:01:49.110 align:middle line:84%
Well, they get delta
times Pt plus 1 star.

01:01:49.110 --> 01:01:52.570 align:middle line:90%


01:01:52.570 --> 01:01:55.797 align:middle line:84%
If I wait till tomorrow, we
know from backward induction

01:01:55.797 --> 01:01:57.380 align:middle line:84%
that the good is
going to be exchanged

01:01:57.380 --> 01:01:59.260 align:middle line:90%
at a price of Pt plus 1 star.

01:01:59.260 --> 01:02:01.120 align:middle line:84%
But I have to discount
that by delta,

01:02:01.120 --> 01:02:03.880 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to be exactly
indifferent if this holds.

01:02:03.880 --> 01:02:06.540 align:middle line:90%


01:02:06.540 --> 01:02:12.520 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to be kind
of really mathy about it,

01:02:12.520 --> 01:02:14.760 align:middle line:84%
we have a solution, but
it's a bit of a mess.

01:02:14.760 --> 01:02:18.980 align:middle line:90%


01:02:18.980 --> 01:02:20.280 align:middle line:90%
So what we know--

01:02:20.280 --> 01:02:27.260 align:middle line:90%


01:02:27.260 --> 01:02:29.400 align:middle line:84%
so how do we compute
our solution?

01:02:29.400 --> 01:02:30.860 align:middle line:90%
We just work backwards.

01:02:30.860 --> 01:02:34.940 align:middle line:84%
We know-- we argued that
the price that's going to be

01:02:34.940 --> 01:02:38.380 align:middle line:84%
determined in period 2n
minus 1 is exactly 0.

01:02:38.380 --> 01:02:40.580 align:middle line:84%
And then we have a
sequence of recursions

01:02:40.580 --> 01:02:45.660 align:middle line:84%
that allow us to compute what
Pt must be if we know Pt plus 1,

01:02:45.660 --> 01:02:48.220 align:middle line:84%
what Pt is if we know
Pt plus 1, and we have

01:02:48.220 --> 01:02:51.220 align:middle line:84%
two different formulas depending
on whether if t is even or odd.

01:02:51.220 --> 01:02:53.380 align:middle line:84%
So technically, we
could start at the end

01:02:53.380 --> 01:02:56.380 align:middle line:90%
and work our way backwards.

01:02:56.380 --> 01:02:59.560 align:middle line:84%
Then we're going to have
some really messy formulas.

01:02:59.560 --> 01:03:00.760 align:middle line:90%
It's going to get ugly.

01:03:00.760 --> 01:03:02.718 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to go
through all the algebra,

01:03:02.718 --> 01:03:05.600 align:middle line:84%
but I just want to show you
what we're going to find,

01:03:05.600 --> 01:03:08.680 align:middle line:84%
so what's the
limit of this game.

01:03:08.680 --> 01:03:20.900 align:middle line:90%


01:03:20.900 --> 01:03:23.620 align:middle line:84%
So we can compute
the full strategy.

01:03:23.620 --> 01:03:27.900 align:middle line:90%
The outcome is going to be--

01:03:27.900 --> 01:03:37.140 align:middle line:84%
well, in period 0, the
seller offers P0 star,

01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:41.940 align:middle line:84%
and the buyer
accepts, where P0 star

01:03:41.940 --> 01:03:43.980 align:middle line:84%
is determined by
starting at the end

01:03:43.980 --> 01:03:46.980 align:middle line:84%
and working all
the way backwards.

01:03:46.980 --> 01:03:49.160 align:middle line:90%
So once again, we see no delay.

01:03:49.160 --> 01:03:52.990 align:middle line:90%


01:03:52.990 --> 01:03:54.410 align:middle line:84%
And this already
came up earlier.

01:03:54.410 --> 01:03:56.170 align:middle line:84%
What's the reason
that we have no delay?

01:03:56.170 --> 01:04:01.070 align:middle line:84%
Why do we not see this
protracted negotiation

01:04:01.070 --> 01:04:02.890 align:middle line:84%
over the price that's
going to be paid?

01:04:02.890 --> 01:04:08.027 align:middle line:90%


01:04:08.027 --> 01:04:09.610 align:middle line:84%
What's kind of missing
from the model?

01:04:09.610 --> 01:04:11.930 align:middle line:84%
I think someone
already brought it up.

01:04:11.930 --> 01:04:12.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:04:12.430 --> 01:04:14.390 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: They have perfect
information so they

01:04:14.390 --> 01:04:15.740 align:middle line:90%
can see what happens in there.

01:04:15.740 --> 01:04:16.490 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

01:04:16.490 --> 01:04:17.948 align:middle line:84%
In reality, when
you're negotiating

01:04:17.948 --> 01:04:20.390 align:middle line:84%
with a price for someone,
you don't know their value,

01:04:20.390 --> 01:04:23.130 align:middle line:84%
v. You don't know exactly how
much they're willing to pay.

01:04:23.130 --> 01:04:25.710 align:middle line:84%
We've made this really
stylized assumption

01:04:25.710 --> 01:04:28.630 align:middle line:84%
that everyone knows
the buyer's willingness

01:04:28.630 --> 01:04:30.430 align:middle line:84%
to pay-- exactly the
most the buyer is

01:04:30.430 --> 01:04:31.440 align:middle line:90%
willing to pay for this.

01:04:31.440 --> 01:04:33.690 align:middle line:84%
That would make-- say you're
negotiating over a house.

01:04:33.690 --> 01:04:36.390 align:middle line:84%
It would be really
easy if you understood

01:04:36.390 --> 01:04:38.710 align:middle line:84%
exactly how much this
family was willing to pay

01:04:38.710 --> 01:04:39.490 align:middle line:90%
to buy this house.

01:04:39.490 --> 01:04:42.330 align:middle line:84%
In reality, we don't know that,
and that's why we see delay.

01:04:42.330 --> 01:04:45.730 align:middle line:84%
But in this benchmark model,
with complete information,

01:04:45.730 --> 01:04:46.775 align:middle line:90%
we see no delay.

01:04:46.775 --> 01:04:49.150 align:middle line:84%
I think this is an example of
where an economic model can

01:04:49.150 --> 01:04:51.360 align:middle line:84%
be useful, even if
it gives us results

01:04:51.360 --> 01:04:52.620 align:middle line:90%
that are counterintuitive.

01:04:52.620 --> 01:04:57.240 align:middle line:84%
Because what it can show
us is it can tell us--

01:04:57.240 --> 01:05:00.100 align:middle line:84%
demonstrate the importance
of certain features.

01:05:00.100 --> 01:05:04.280 align:middle line:84%
What this tells us is that the
driver of delay in bargaining

01:05:04.280 --> 01:05:08.388 align:middle line:84%
is really incomplete information
about people's preferences.

01:05:08.388 --> 01:05:09.180 align:middle line:90%
How do we see this?

01:05:09.180 --> 01:05:11.540 align:middle line:84%
Well, we see if there were
no incomplete information,

01:05:11.540 --> 01:05:12.580 align:middle line:90%
we would have no delay.

01:05:12.580 --> 01:05:14.740 align:middle line:84%
And this shows us that something
must be missing from the model.

01:05:14.740 --> 01:05:16.820 align:middle line:84%
And I guess we suspect it's
incomplete information.

01:05:16.820 --> 01:05:18.070 align:middle line:90%
And we can see that the later.

01:05:18.070 --> 01:05:19.720 align:middle line:90%
We can model that later.

01:05:19.720 --> 01:05:24.220 align:middle line:84%
I want to describe,
though, briefly,

01:05:24.220 --> 01:05:33.520 align:middle line:84%
what's the limiting
case if n is very large?

01:05:33.520 --> 01:05:35.160 align:middle line:84%
And I explained
this is what we're

01:05:35.160 --> 01:05:37.960 align:middle line:84%
going to converge to if we
looked at the infinite horizon

01:05:37.960 --> 01:05:40.060 align:middle line:90%
model.

01:05:40.060 --> 01:05:54.320 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that P0
star is going to be 1 over 1

01:05:54.320 --> 01:06:01.120 align:middle line:90%
plus delta times v.

01:06:01.120 --> 01:06:03.383 align:middle line:90%
Let's try to interpret this.

01:06:03.383 --> 01:06:04.300 align:middle line:90%
And this is the case--

01:06:04.300 --> 01:06:06.940 align:middle line:84%
I should say in our model,
the seller moves first.

01:06:06.940 --> 01:06:15.040 align:middle line:90%


01:06:15.040 --> 01:06:17.660 align:middle line:84%
What if we had said had
the buyer proposed first?

01:06:17.660 --> 01:06:24.000 align:middle line:90%


01:06:24.000 --> 01:06:26.440 align:middle line:84%
We can also compute
what P0 star is.

01:06:26.440 --> 01:06:27.820 align:middle line:90%
Any guesses if the buyer--

01:06:27.820 --> 01:06:29.440 align:middle line:84%
So we're still
looking at the limit

01:06:29.440 --> 01:06:33.560 align:middle line:84%
where we could, in principle,
negotiate for a very long time.

01:06:33.560 --> 01:06:35.320 align:middle line:84%
This is the price that
the seller is going

01:06:35.320 --> 01:06:38.000 align:middle line:90%
to offer if they propose first.

01:06:38.000 --> 01:06:40.780 align:middle line:84%
What if the buyer
got to propose first?

01:06:40.780 --> 01:06:45.712 align:middle line:84%
Any guesses on whether this
price would be higher or lower?

01:06:45.712 --> 01:06:47.170 align:middle line:84%
Again, we can do
the algebra, but I

01:06:47.170 --> 01:06:49.930 align:middle line:84%
think it's a lot better to
think through these things

01:06:49.930 --> 01:06:52.570 align:middle line:90%
intuitively.

01:06:52.570 --> 01:06:55.070 align:middle line:84%
Would you rather go first
or not in this negotiation?

01:06:55.070 --> 01:06:59.930 align:middle line:90%


01:06:59.930 --> 01:07:02.310 align:middle line:90%
Any intuition?

01:07:02.310 --> 01:07:05.890 align:middle line:90%


01:07:05.890 --> 01:07:09.090 align:middle line:90%
It's not so easy?

01:07:09.090 --> 01:07:13.450 align:middle line:84%
It turns out going first
is generally an advantage.

01:07:13.450 --> 01:07:15.570 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is
that if I go first,

01:07:15.570 --> 01:07:19.850 align:middle line:84%
I can take advantage of the fact
that, yes, we both experience

01:07:19.850 --> 01:07:20.950 align:middle line:90%
a cost of delay.

01:07:20.950 --> 01:07:22.650 align:middle line:84%
But, in particular,
the other party

01:07:22.650 --> 01:07:24.270 align:middle line:90%
experiences a cost of delay.

01:07:24.270 --> 01:07:26.450 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, they must
be willing to accept offers

01:07:26.450 --> 01:07:29.030 align:middle line:90%
that aren't that good for them.

01:07:29.030 --> 01:07:30.942 align:middle line:84%
So if I go first,
I can say, look,

01:07:30.942 --> 01:07:32.650 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give you
a pretty bad offer,

01:07:32.650 --> 01:07:34.450 align:middle line:84%
and I know you're going
to have to accept it.

01:07:34.450 --> 01:07:35.890 align:middle line:84%
Because if you don't
accept it, you're

01:07:35.890 --> 01:07:37.570 align:middle line:84%
going to have to pay
the cost of delay.

01:07:37.570 --> 01:07:39.715 align:middle line:84%
And this gives kind of
a first mover advantage.

01:07:39.715 --> 01:07:41.090 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out
what we're going

01:07:41.090 --> 01:07:46.890 align:middle line:84%
to get here is P0 star is
going to be delta over 1

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:48.330 align:middle line:90%
plus delta times v.

01:07:48.330 --> 01:07:52.250 align:middle line:84%
So if the buyer goes first, the
price the buyer is going to get

01:07:52.250 --> 01:07:55.235 align:middle line:84%
is going to be strictly
smaller by a factor of delta

01:07:55.235 --> 01:07:56.610 align:middle line:84%
relative to what
the seller would

01:07:56.610 --> 01:07:59.290 align:middle line:90%
get if the seller went first.

01:07:59.290 --> 01:08:02.070 align:middle line:84%
And here, we can see
the role of discounting.

01:08:02.070 --> 01:08:04.270 align:middle line:90%
What happens if delta goes to 1?

01:08:04.270 --> 01:08:08.050 align:middle line:90%


01:08:08.050 --> 01:08:12.450 align:middle line:84%
If delta goes to 1,
the price the seller

01:08:12.450 --> 01:08:17.170 align:middle line:84%
gets if they go first
converges to v over 2.

01:08:17.170 --> 01:08:19.770 align:middle line:84%
And the price the buyer
gets if they go first also

01:08:19.770 --> 01:08:21.609 align:middle line:90%
converges to v over 2.

01:08:21.609 --> 01:08:24.250 align:middle line:84%
So we see as delta goes
to 1, and the players

01:08:24.250 --> 01:08:28.270 align:middle line:84%
become very impatient, the first
mover advantage disappears.

01:08:28.270 --> 01:08:43.560 align:middle line:90%


01:08:43.560 --> 01:08:45.560 align:middle line:84%
Can someone see-- if they
can provide intuition,

01:08:45.560 --> 01:08:50.260 align:middle line:84%
why does the first mover
advantage disappear

01:08:50.260 --> 01:08:52.080 align:middle line:90%
if delta goes to 1?

01:08:52.080 --> 01:08:52.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:08:52.580 --> 01:08:54.497 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Just to make
sure, when delta goes 1,

01:08:54.497 --> 01:08:56.319 align:middle line:84%
they both are very
patient [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:56.319 --> 01:08:57.319 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

01:08:57.319 --> 01:08:59.420 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
if they don't

01:08:59.420 --> 01:09:02.420 align:middle line:90%
care about waiting [INAUDIBLE].

01:09:02.420 --> 01:09:03.140 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

01:09:03.140 --> 01:09:06.740 align:middle line:84%
If we get very, very patient,
then it shouldn't really

01:09:06.740 --> 01:09:08.660 align:middle line:90%
matter who goes first.

01:09:08.660 --> 01:09:11.000 align:middle line:84%
Because tomorrow the other
person gets to go first.

01:09:11.000 --> 01:09:13.380 align:middle line:84%
And tomorrow isn't that
much different than today

01:09:13.380 --> 01:09:15.200 align:middle line:90%
if delta gets very close to 1.

01:09:15.200 --> 01:09:17.805 align:middle line:84%
Remember, another interpretation
of delta going to 1,

01:09:17.805 --> 01:09:19.180 align:middle line:84%
one interpretation
is the players

01:09:19.180 --> 01:09:20.979 align:middle line:90%
are literally more patient.

01:09:20.979 --> 01:09:23.420 align:middle line:84%
Another interpretation
is they can make offers

01:09:23.420 --> 01:09:25.300 align:middle line:90%
in very rapid succession.

01:09:25.300 --> 01:09:28.700 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that the reason
period 1 is not very discounted

01:09:28.700 --> 01:09:31.649 align:middle line:84%
relative to period 0 is that
the offer made in period 1

01:09:31.649 --> 01:09:33.399 align:middle line:84%
happens right after
the offer in period 0.

01:09:33.399 --> 01:09:35.040 align:middle line:84%
They happen in really
quick succession.

01:09:35.040 --> 01:09:37.020 align:middle line:84%
And intuitively, if
we're making alternating

01:09:37.020 --> 01:09:39.020 align:middle line:84%
offers every millisecond,
it shouldn't really

01:09:39.020 --> 01:09:40.158 align:middle line:90%
matter who goes first.

01:09:40.158 --> 01:09:41.450 align:middle line:90%
And that's exactly what we see.

01:09:41.450 --> 01:09:44.069 align:middle line:84%
The first mover
advantage disappears.

01:09:44.069 --> 01:09:46.830 align:middle line:84%
Let me just to
conclude, try to derive,

01:09:46.830 --> 01:09:49.930 align:middle line:84%
give a heuristic derivation
of these limiting formulas,

01:09:49.930 --> 01:09:51.490 align:middle line:90%
and then we'll conclude.

01:09:51.490 --> 01:10:07.230 align:middle line:84%
So let's-- so how can we
try to solve for, let's say,

01:10:07.230 --> 01:10:08.050 align:middle line:90%
the first formula?

01:10:08.050 --> 01:10:12.650 align:middle line:84%
So let's say the
seller proposes.

01:10:12.650 --> 01:10:16.750 align:middle line:90%


01:10:16.750 --> 01:10:33.150 align:middle line:84%
Then what we expect is if the
seller proposes, and we're

01:10:33.150 --> 01:10:36.630 align:middle line:84%
taking the limit as
the number of periods

01:10:36.630 --> 01:10:40.550 align:middle line:84%
get very large, then the
difference between period

01:10:40.550 --> 01:10:44.950 align:middle line:84%
0 and period 2, we think that
it should get very, very close.

01:10:44.950 --> 01:10:48.550 align:middle line:84%
Because in period 2, we have
a billion periods to go.

01:10:48.550 --> 01:10:51.510 align:middle line:84%
In period 0, we have a
billion plus 2 periods to go.

01:10:51.510 --> 01:10:54.630 align:middle line:84%
Since it's a billion, they
should be pretty similar.

01:10:54.630 --> 01:10:55.930 align:middle line:90%
This is a heuristic derivation.

01:10:55.930 --> 01:10:59.230 align:middle line:84%
And now let's plug this in
and see what we can get.

01:10:59.230 --> 01:11:05.070 align:middle line:84%
So we get P0 star
from this recursion

01:11:05.070 --> 01:11:11.450 align:middle line:84%
is equal to 1 minus delta times
v plus delta times P1 star.

01:11:11.450 --> 01:11:14.790 align:middle line:90%


01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:21.230 align:middle line:84%
But then using this formula,
we know that P1 star

01:11:21.230 --> 01:11:26.850 align:middle line:90%
equals delta times P2 star.

01:11:26.850 --> 01:11:30.710 align:middle line:84%
I'm just applying this
formula with t equals 0,

01:11:30.710 --> 01:11:33.470 align:middle line:84%
and then I'm applying this
formula with t equals 1.

01:11:33.470 --> 01:11:38.560 align:middle line:84%
So now let's plug
this into here,

01:11:38.560 --> 01:11:48.120 align:middle line:84%
and we get P0 star equals 1
minus delta v plus delta squared

01:11:48.120 --> 01:11:48.980 align:middle line:90%
P2 star.

01:11:48.980 --> 01:11:56.240 align:middle line:90%


01:11:56.240 --> 01:12:00.120 align:middle line:84%
Now, in general, P0 star and P2
star are going to be different.

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:03.592 align:middle line:84%
But in the limit as n goes
to infinity, P0 and P2 star

01:12:03.592 --> 01:12:05.300 align:middle line:84%
are going to look
really, really similar.

01:12:05.300 --> 01:12:07.240 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is
that, again, if there's

01:12:07.240 --> 01:12:10.180 align:middle line:84%
many, many periods ahead of us,
a billion periods and a billion

01:12:10.180 --> 01:12:12.180 align:middle line:84%
plus 2 periods, it should
be basically the same.

01:12:12.180 --> 01:12:15.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's squint and
make this P0 star.

01:12:15.780 --> 01:12:18.640 align:middle line:90%


01:12:18.640 --> 01:12:20.480 align:middle line:90%
Now let's try to solve this.

01:12:20.480 --> 01:12:24.500 align:middle line:90%
We bring this to the other side.

01:12:24.500 --> 01:12:34.120 align:middle line:84%
So we get 1 minus delta
squared P0 star equals

01:12:34.120 --> 01:12:40.840 align:middle line:84%
1 minus delta v. And now there's
one kind of trick that I think

01:12:40.840 --> 01:12:43.220 align:middle line:84%
is kind of good to
remember on problem sets.

01:12:43.220 --> 01:12:46.740 align:middle line:84%
It's not so clear if
we solve for this,

01:12:46.740 --> 01:12:51.920 align:middle line:84%
we get P0 star equals 1 minus
delta over 1 minus delta

01:12:51.920 --> 01:12:54.960 align:middle line:90%
squared times v. Uh-oh.

01:12:54.960 --> 01:12:58.500 align:middle line:84%
That's not the formula we
got, but it simplifies.

01:12:58.500 --> 01:12:59.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:12:59.000 --> 01:12:59.720 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Factor.

01:12:59.720 --> 01:13:01.040 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: We can factor, right?

01:13:01.040 --> 01:13:05.360 align:middle line:84%
1 minus delta squared is just 1
minus delta times 1 plus delta.

01:13:05.360 --> 01:13:08.840 align:middle line:84%
The 1 minus deltas cancel,
and we exactly get 1 over 1

01:13:08.840 --> 01:13:13.450 align:middle line:84%
plus delta times v.
So again, I don't

01:13:13.450 --> 01:13:15.200 align:middle line:84%
want to make the problem
set for the exams

01:13:15.200 --> 01:13:17.360 align:middle line:84%
all about doing algebra,
but this is one trick

01:13:17.360 --> 01:13:18.660 align:middle line:90%
that I think is good to know.

01:13:18.660 --> 01:13:21.515 align:middle line:90%


01:13:21.515 --> 01:13:24.140 align:middle line:84%
Let me stop there-- a little bit
early, but I think we're good.

01:13:24.140 --> 01:13:28.000 align:middle line:84%
And we'll continue on Tuesday
with subgame perfect Nash

01:13:28.000 --> 01:13:28.840 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:30.710 align:middle line:90%
I'll see you then.

01:13:30.710 --> 01:13:34.000 align:middle line:90%